window.SEARCH=[{"k":"Field note","t":"Done Should Be Calculated, Not Typed","x":"On most status reports, done is a number somebody types. It drifts up, because nobody wants the argument , and then sits at 95 percent for months ( the last 5 percent ). Define done by the evidence that proves it. A wall is done when its inspection is signed, its deficiencies are closed and its test record is filed. A system is done when every part of it is. Nobody types it. It follows from the records. What changes Arguments move to records. \"It's done\" becomes \"which signature is missing?\" ( signatures ). Partial progress stops being a guess. Count the evidence that exists against the evidence required. Closeout shows up early, as missing evidence on items everyone thought were finished. Rollups can't drift. A floor is done when its rooms are, and a building when its floors are ( one breakdown ). Try it on one area. List what has to be true for each item to count as done, and mark only what you can point to. The first report will show less progress than last week's. That's the accurate one.","u":"/all.html#done-should-be-calculated"},{"k":"Field note","t":"Every Open Item Should Explain Itself","x":"Ask why a task isn't finished and the answer usually takes three phone calls. The crew is waiting on scaffold. Scaffold is waiting on a permit. The permit is waiting on a signature from someone on holiday . Somebody knows each of those links. None of them is written down where the next person can follow it. Follow the chain to a name For every open item, record what it is waiting on, then what that is waiting on, until you reach a person and a date. \"Why isn't this done?\" then has an answer anyone can read in a minute, without asking around . Two things fall out. Every chain ends at a named person, so nothing waits on \"them\". And chains that end at the same blocker show where clearing one thing frees the most work . The chains also show where handoffs are slowing things down, and how long the news took to arrive. It works because everything blocking work is the same kind of thing : a condition that has to be true first. Try it in the next coordination meeting. For each red item, keep asking what it's waiting on until you reach a name, and write the chain down. Most meetings never get past the first link.","u":"/all.html#every-open-item-should-explain-itself"},{"k":"Field note","t":"Price Every Risk in Days","x":"A heat map puts a risk in a red square. It doesn't say what the risk would do to the finish date, so the meeting argues about colours. Price it instead. Run the schedule many times with realistic variation in durations and deliveries, once with the risk and once without. The difference in the finish date is what that risk costs, in days, and in dollars at your daily cost of delay. The list sorts itself. A likely risk on a path with float may cost nothing. An unlikely one on a path that is often critical may cost weeks. Timing counts. The same delay costs more just before a closing window . Responses get a price too. If a mitigation costs less than the days it saves, do it. Assumptions are risks. Price the ones you haven't checked . Severe, irreversible losses still need judgment on top of the number ( reversibility ). Most large jobs already run this kind of analysis once, for the owner, then file it . Run it monthly and use the result to rank the risk register.","u":"/all.html#price-every-risk-in-days"},{"k":"Field note","t":"A Crane Is a Predecessor Too","x":"Two lifts that need the same crane can't happen at once. That tie is as firm as rebar before concrete. Most schedules keep logic in one place and resources in another, and reconcile them at the end, if at all. So the schedule shows both lifts on Tuesday, and the crane operator decides which one waits. One kind of link Treat shared capacity as a link between activities: these two need the same crane, the same welder , the same room, the same outage . Then the sequence you see is the one the site will run. It's the same idea as everything being a constraint : capacity is one more condition that has to be free first. It explains float that disappears on site : the logic said eight days, but the crane didn't. It also explains why the critical path often runs through one busy resource instead of through the logic. And crews are shared capacity too. Try it on the next three-week lookahead. List every crane, hoist, specialist and shutdown window, and draw a line between activities that share one. The conflicts are hard to miss once they're drawn.","u":"/all.html#a-crane-is-a-predecessor"},{"k":"Field note","t":"Write the Site Rules Once","x":"No hot work within 10 metres of fuel. Two people for confined space entry. Hold for the inspector before backfill. These rules are in a binder, a toolbox talk and the superintendent's head. The plan doesn't know any of them. So a lookahead can put welding next to a fuel delivery, and someone has to notice. Rules the plan can check Write each rule as a check against the plan: which activities it applies to, and what has to be true before they go ahead. Run the checks every time the plan changes, and a conflict shows up on the lookahead days before the crew arrives. Rules written once stop depending on who's on shift, and they don't get lost at a handoff . Change a rule in one place and every future plan follows the new version, the same way procedures written as steps do. Requirements like the two-hour wall can be checked the same way for the life of the building. Start with the rules that guard against the worst outcomes , and the hold points that need a signature to release. Each rule is a constraint like any other. Try it with the five rules behind your last few near misses. Check next week's lookahead against them by hand and count the conflicts.","u":"/all.html#write-the-site-rules-once"},{"k":"Field note","t":"The Last 5 Percent","x":"Punch lists, commissioning, turnover documents and closeout often take a third of the calendar. They get planned as if they were the last 5 percent of the work, and they start getting managed when the crews are leaving. By then the people who knew the systems have moved on, the records are scattered across the files , and every remaining item depends on somebody else . Start it on day one Give every system a turnover package and an owner from the start. Fill it as the work is done: test records, certificates, signed inspections , field notes . Close deficiencies as they appear , each with a photo or short video , instead of collecting 1,400 at the final walkthrough. Schedule commissioning as work , with durations and logic, not as a single diamond at the end. Don't report 95 percent as nearly done. A green status at 95 percent hides the slowest third of the job, and the bad news arrives at the end. Use the as-built schedules from past jobs to see how long closeout took. It is usually far longer than the plan said. The last 5 percent is where the owner forms a final opinion of the job, and where the retention money is held.","u":"/all.html#the-last-five-percent"},{"k":"Field note","t":"Keep the As-Built Schedule","x":"At closeout, the start and finish of every activity gets archived with the project files and is never opened again. It is the best estimating data a contractor owns: real production rates, on its own jobs, with its own crews, in its own climate. The next estimate starts from published rates and someone's memory instead. That's why the same activities come in light, job after job. What to keep Durations and quantities. Planned against as-built for every activity, with the quantity installed, so you get a rate: units per crew-day. Why the big gaps happened. The weather days , the blocked shifts , the rework. A slow activity with a known cause tells you something different from one with no explanation. Who did the work. Crews that stayed together ran faster, and some activities depended on one person . You can only compare jobs if they share a breakdown structure , and the data is only cheap to keep if it was captured once while the work happened. Kept that way, it becomes your own reference class. Estimates start from what happened here before, ranges come from real spreads instead of guesses, and float sits on durations you can defend. Forecasts can be scored against it. It also shows how long the last 5 percent takes on your jobs.","u":"/all.html#keep-the-as-built-schedule"},{"k":"Field note","t":"The Cost of Asking","x":"Every site has a price for asking a question. Part of it is time: finding the person who knows, then waiting for them to answer. Part of it is social: looking like you should already know. When the price is high, people guess, and some of those guesses get built. What makes asking expensive Nobody obvious to ask. The requirement is buried in a document the electrician will never see ( the two-hour wall ). The person who can decide is away and nobody else can sign ( delegation , signatures ). Asking looks bad. Where people expect blame, they stay quiet ( problem employees ). Quiet sites ask less ( inside jokes ). The person who asks awkward questions in meetings gets treated as a nuisance ( odd people ). The answer comes too late. An RFI that takes three weeks teaches crews to stop sending them. Every handoff adds a wait ( handoffs ). Outsiders don't know who to ask. A consultant spends a month finding out what the site team knows ( consultants ). Make it cheap Track two numbers: how many questions get asked, and how long they take to answer. More questions is a good sign. Slow answers are the problem. Give instructions with their tolerances so fewer questions are needed. Answer the ones that block the most work first ( the morning question ). Treat an unanswered question like any other constraint : it holds up work until it's cleared. Bad news has to travel up. Questions have to travel down and across. Both slow down at every handoff.","u":"/all.html#the-cost-of-asking"},{"k":"Field note","t":"Count the Handoffs","x":"Schedules count tasks. Most of what goes wrong happens between tasks, where work, information or authority passes from one person, crew, company or system to the next. Each handoff can drop something: The requirement. The fire rating is in the design basis, the detail and the QC record, and missing from what the electrician sees ten years later ( the two-hour wall ). The item. A spool that changes hands six times can go missing at any of them ( material ). The authority. A signer on holiday stops the next crew ( delegation , signatures ). The intent. \"ASAP\" arrives without the tolerance behind it ( tolerances ). The context. A crew gets split up, or a consultant leaves with what they learned ( crews , consultants ). Time. Every handoff adds days before bad news reaches the plan . Fewer, then explicit Count handoffs per work package the way you count activities. Remove the ones that exist only because the same data is kept in separate copies ( one breakdown , capture once ). Make the rest explicit: a scan at the handoff, a signature with a name, steps that travel with the work . Suppliers are a handoff too, and the one you control least. The whiteboard and the schedule are a handoff that happens every morning. Named people make the handoffs that depend on one person visible before they fail. A schedule with fewer tasks isn't simpler if it has more handoffs.","u":"/all.html#count-the-handoffs"},{"k":"Field note","t":"How Long Bad News Takes to Reach the Plan","x":"Every problem has two dates: when someone on site first saw it, and when it showed up in the plan. Call the gap bad-news latency. On most jobs it runs to days or weeks, and for all of that time decisions are being made from a plan that is wrong. Nobody tracks it, so nobody manages it. Measuring it is simple: log when each new constraint was first seen and when it entered the plan, and watch the median gap week to week. Where the days go Seeing it and saying it. Quiet crews don't pass things on ( inside jokes ), people who expect blame keep quiet ( problem employees ), and small injuries go unreported ( minor injuries ). Writing it down. If capture takes more than a minute, it waits for Friday. A tap on the timesheet , a phone and a thirty-second video record it on the spot, once . Getting it into the plan. The whiteboard knows by 6 a.m. The schedule finds out at the monthly update, too late for the morning question . Partners. A supplier who has been given padded dates sits on their own delays ( suppliers ). Reporting. Unsigned green status and stale baselines hide what someone already knows. Why the delay is expensive A week of delay on most news costs about a week. On a closing window it can cost a season. On a decision that can't be reversed , the news may arrive after the decision is made. And the forecast range can't narrow faster than new problems reach the plan. Most fixes in these notes shorten one stage of the delay. Measuring the whole delay shows which stage is slowest on your job.","u":"/all.html#bad-news-latency"},{"k":"Field note","t":"Inside Jokes Are a Good Sign","x":"A crew that draws cartoons on the trailer whiteboard is a crew that talks. The GIF about the sticking valve in Sector 4 does more to keep people away from that valve than the line in last month's report. Worry about the quiet site. If nobody jokes, nobody is passing on what they know either. If the whiteboard is full of stick figures, leave it up.","u":"/all.html#memes-are-meaningful"},{"k":"Field note","t":"Put Odd People Where They Fit","x":"The person who annoys everyone by checking every bolt callout is the one who catches the wrong grade before the pour. The one who argues in every meeting is the one asking what happens when the crane goes down. Most difficult people have one habit that is very valuable in the right job and tiresome in the wrong one. Don't train it out of them. Move them to where it pays.","u":"/all.html#embrace-the-weirdos"},{"k":"Field note","t":"Most Problem Employees Are Reacting to the Job","x":"Give someone who looks checked out a ready work front, the right tools and a straight answer when they ask, and most of them start working well. A lot of bad attitudes are reasonable responses to a badly run job. A few people do damage on purpose: they hide information, misreport progress, or take safety shortcuts with other people's lives. Remove them quickly. Everyone else is watching what you tolerate.","u":"/all.html#most-people-are-redeemable"},{"k":"Field note","t":"Match the Rigour to the Consequence","x":"A temporary laydown pad does not need the paperwork of a pressure boundary. Spending four days perfecting it costs four days on work that drives the finish. Decide early which work carries real consequence: life safety, pressure retention, critical tie-ins, long-lead equipment. Hold that to the highest standard. For everything else, the standard is that it works, passes inspection and doesn't hold up the next crew. Say which is which out loud, so crews don't have to guess.","u":"/all.html#not-everything-is-a-nuclear-valve"},{"k":"Field note","t":"Give Instructions With Tolerances","x":"Drawings say ±5 mm. Instructions say \"ASAP\". The crew can't tell a hold point from a favour, so they treat everything as urgent and get worn out by work that wasn't. Give the range you'd accept: When: \"Next week. A couple of days late is fine.\" Where: \"Somewhere near the break trailer that doesn't block the door.\" What happens if it goes wrong: \"If you nick the phone line it's a repair call, not an injury.\" People make good calls when they know how much room they have.","u":"/all.html#clear-expectations-include-tolerances"},{"k":"Field note","t":"Define the Activity, Not Just Its Duration","x":"\"Hydrovac the copper lines, 3 days\" invites three days of digging whether you need it or not. \"Hydrovac until you can see the layout, a day at most\" tells the crew when they're done. For uncertain work, set the stopping point and the limit. The duration follows. Without a stopping point, work expands to fill the bar.","u":"/all.html#activities-have-ranges"},{"k":"Field note","t":"Check Your Float","x":"Float is only as good as the logic under it. One missing tie and an activity shows eight days of slack it doesn't have. One padded duration and a path looks critical when it isn't. Both errors cost you: the first gets ignored until it bites, the second gets resources it doesn't need. Before trusting a float number, walk the logic behind it with the people doing the work.","u":"/all.html#float-is-never-accurate"},{"k":"Field note","t":"Not All Milestones Matter Equally","x":"A submittal date and first energization get the same diamond on the schedule. They are not the same. Miss the freeze-up window and you lose a season. Miss an internal rough-in target by three days with the next trade not mobilized, and nothing happens. Name the three or four milestones the job depends on. Protect those. If every date is treated as critical, crews learn that none are.","u":"/all.html#milestones-arent-all-equal"},{"k":"Field note","t":"Treat Suppliers Like Part of the Team","x":"Suppliers know when your \"drop-dead\" date has two weeks of padding in it. Lie to them and they stop telling you about the problems in their shop until it's too late to work around them. Give them the actual install date and where you have slack. Call them in when a detail looks wrong, before it becomes a string of RFIs. Clear laydown before the truck arrives. When something goes wrong, the supplier who trusts you finds a way. The one you've mistreated sends you the contract.","u":"/all.html#suppliers-are-part-of-your-team"},{"k":"Field note","t":"Use Your Own People Before Consultants","x":"A consultant at $350 an hour spends the first month learning what your superintendent already knows: which permits are slow, which substation feeds what, why the north yard floods in spring. Then the report repeats what the site team said in the first interview. Your people live with the result. Consultants are worth it for narrow, rare problems: a forensic metallurgist, a one-off regulatory witness. For running the work, invest in the people already there.","u":"/all.html#home-grown-talent"},{"k":"Field note","t":"Give Everyone a Desk and a Locker","x":"If someone keeps their tools in their truck and does paperwork on a gangbox, you've told them they're temporary. Most people act accordingly. A shared desk, a chair, working Wi-Fi and a locker that locks are cheap. Include the cleaners, yard hands and apprentices. The people who keep the site running notice who got a space and who didn't.","u":"/all.html#everyone-needs-a-desk-and-a-locker"},{"k":"Field note","t":"Collect Status Through the Timesheet","x":"Status forms are optional, so they don't get filled in. Timesheets always do, because people want to be paid. Put one question on the timesheet for each task: done, partial or blocked. Blocked shows that task's open constraints to pick from, or takes a photo of a new one. It adds a minute at the end of the shift, and the schedule is current every night.","u":"/all.html#timesheet-extortion"},{"k":"Field note","t":"The Two-Hour Wall","x":"The fire code requires a two-hour wall. That requirement gets copied into the design basis, a wall type, a firestop detail, a QC record and a commissioning check. Then the project closes out and the copies scatter. Ten years later an electrician cores a cable tray through it with no idea it's rated. Keep the requirement as one record that design, construction and operations all point to. Then the person holding the drill can find out why the wall is there.","u":"/all.html#requirements-and-data-loss"},{"k":"Field note","t":"Recurring Work Gets Lost in One Long Bar","x":"Weekly scaffold inspections show up as one 200-day bar. The bar can't be late, so nobody notices when this week's inspection is. The tag expires Thursday. Friday, three crews have no legal platform, and their timesheets record low productivity. Schedule each inspection as its own item with an expiry, and on Wednesday you can see whose platform is about to lapse.","u":"/all.html#recurring-work-is-invisible"},{"k":"Field note","t":"Phones Cost Less Than Crew Time","x":"A crew-hour costs more than a phone. Yet field data still gets written on paper, carried to the trailer and typed in after the crew goes home. Company phones are allowed on almost every site, so policy isn't the obstacle. The obstacle is software that works with gloves on, in the rain, in ten seconds. The timesheet is the right place to start, because everyone opens it.","u":"/all.html#smartphones-are-cheap"},{"k":"Field note","t":"Add Reversibility to Risk","x":"Safety learned to put attention on what can kill people, not on what happens often. Project risk can borrow that. Risk matrices score probability and severity. Add reversibility. A cost overrun spread over three years has many points where you can step in. A contract clause signed in an afternoon may have none. Where the loss is severe and can't be undone, treat it as if it will happen, and put a senior person on the decision.","u":"/all.html#stky-cubed"},{"k":"Field note","t":"Track Minor Injuries Too","x":"Minor cuts and strains go unreported because reporting takes longer than the bandage. If logging one takes more than ten seconds, you'll have no data. Once you have it, the patterns are obvious: the same knife, the same task, the same corner of the site. Small injuries don't predict fatalities, but they do repeat for the same reasons, and the fixes are usually cheap.","u":"/all.html#a-thousand-papercuts"},{"k":"Field note","t":"Crews Are More Than Headcount","x":"The plan prices six welders at a rate. A crew that has worked together all season outperforms six welders who met on Monday, at the same rate. That difference is in who knows whose signals, and who doesn't need to be told the sequence. Resource levelling can't see it. Splitting a good crew is a schedule decision, even though it never appears in the schedule.","u":"/all.html#people-matter"},{"k":"Field note","t":"Plan With Weather Data","x":"Weather is the only project risk with a free, public, daily probabilistic forecast. Most schedules still plan a February pour like a June one. Thirty years of records tell you how many workable pour days February usually has. Plan on that range. The ten-day forecast tells you when Thursday's wind will exceed the lift limit. Today it lives on the superintendent's phone, and the schedule finds out from the delay notice.","u":"/all.html#weather-is-a-bitch"},{"k":"Field note","t":"Count How Fast You Find New Problems","x":"You can't list your unknown unknowns. You can count how many new constraints turn up each week. That number should fall as the job matures. It also falls when people stop looking. So count the looking too: walkdowns, assumption checks, design reviews. If new problems fall while the checks hold steady, you're learning. If both fall, the surprises are still coming.","u":"/all.html#unknown-unknowns"},{"k":"Field note","t":"Track Material at Every Handoff","x":"A pipe spool is fabricated, shipped, received, staged and installed, and each of those steps is in a different system. Finding it still takes three phone calls. Couriers solved this decades ago with a scan at every handoff. If a crew has to ask where something is, the tracking is missing a step.","u":"/all.html#where-is-my-material"},{"k":"Field note","t":"Diagrams Should Come From the Plan","x":"A roadmap drawn by hand has boxes without dates, arrows without logic and no owners. Nobody can check it, so nobody can prove it wrong. That's why it survives. Draw the picture from the plan. Then it changes when the plan changes, and it can't drift into a second version of the project.","u":"/all.html#roadmaps-and-visio"},{"k":"Field note","t":"Short Videos Settle Disputes","x":"Half of all claims are arguments about what the work area looked like on a given date. Thirty seconds of video taken that day ends the argument. The phones are already on site. Attach the clip to the item it relates to, and the evidence is filed before anyone knows they'll need it.","u":"/all.html#a-video-is-worth"},{"k":"Field note","t":"Make Signatures Quick and Traceable","x":"A hold-point signature can mean a drive across site, a binder and a wet pen. So people batch them, or sign without looking, and a signature stops meaning much. Make signing take five seconds on a phone, with the signer's name and time recorded permanently. Easy to give, impossible to deny.","u":"/all.html#signatures-should-be-easy"},{"k":"Field note","t":"Record Delegation With an End Date","x":"Who can sign for whom is usually known to two people and an out-of-office reply. When the signer goes on holiday, the job finds out at the first stalled hold point. Write delegation down: from whom, to whom, for what, until when. Then absences stop stopping work.","u":"/all.html#delegation-made-easy"},{"k":"Field note","t":"Write Procedures as Steps, Not PDFs","x":"A procedure in a PDF is advice. The crew carries out a sequence of steps, checks, hold points and qualifications, whatever the document looks like. Write it as structured steps and each run produces a record you can check. Change the procedure once and the next run uses the new version, instead of the one printed in somebody's binder.","u":"/all.html#programmable-procedures"},{"k":"Field note","t":"Which Constraint Should We Clear Today?","x":"Status meetings ask what happened yesterday. The useful question is which blocked item, if cleared today, frees up the most work. Constraint logs sort by date raised or by owner, never by work released. Good superintendents rank them in their heads at 5:30 a.m. Put that ranking somewhere the whole team can see it.","u":"/all.html#the-morning-question"},{"k":"Field note","t":"The Critical Path Changes","x":"Run a schedule through a thousand simulations of weather and deliveries and a different path is critical in about a third of them. The printed critical path is one of those outcomes. It also moves with every update, and crews get pulled toward whatever went red this month. A path that is critical in 60 runs out of 100 needs attention every month, whatever the printout says.","u":"/all.html#the-critical-path-is-a-coin-flip"},{"k":"Field note","t":"Give Assumptions a Recheck Date","x":"Ground conditions, vendor capacity, \"the permit will renew\": written down at sanction and never read again. An assumption is a risk nobody has checked yet. Assumptions go stale at different rates. Soil conditions hold for years; a vendor's shop loading changes weekly. Give each one an owner and a recheck date that matches how fast it can change.","u":"/all.html#assumed-true-until-checked"},{"k":"Field note","t":"Baselines Go Stale","x":"Two winters and forty change orders after sanction, the job is still measured against the sanction-day plan, and the gap is called variance. Rebaselining feels like admitting failure, so it happens late, and every report in between measures distance from a plan nobody believes. Keep every forecast. The baseline is the one with the contract attached. Score how accurate each forecast turned out to be, and rebaselining becomes routine.","u":"/all.html#baselines-rot"},{"k":"Field note","t":"The Whiteboard and the Schedule Drift Apart","x":"The schedule has five thousand activities. The site runs off a whiteboard three weeks deep, updated at 6 a.m. Both are needed: the contract needs the full record, and the crew needs this week. The trouble starts when the whiteboard becomes the working plan and never feeds back. Within a season, the schedule describes a different job. Treat them as two views of the same plan, and make changes on the board flow back.","u":"/all.html#nobody-reads-the-schedule"},{"k":"Field note","t":"Everything Is a Constraint","x":"A predecessor, a missing spool, an unsigned permit, a crane booked elsewhere, wind over the lift limit, a room too crowded to work in, a dose budget nearly spent. Seven systems manage them. They're all the same thing: a condition that must be true before work can start. CPM made logic ties the main constraint because that was what 1960s computers could handle. Ask a superintendent what stopped work this week and it is rarely a predecessor. Dates are outputs If everything is a constraint, the schedule is what the constraints produce, not the plan itself. Each one has the same fields: what it blocks, what clears it, who owns it, when it expires. A permit and a weather window differ only in how they get cleared. Keep them in one place and you can ask each morning which ones to clear to free the most work. I don't know yet whether one system can hold all seven types well. The cost of keeping them in seven is clear.","u":"/all.html#everything-is-a-constraint"},{"k":"Field note","t":"Watch for Closing Windows","x":"A pour needs three dry days. The crane leaves in October. In-water work stops when the fish return. None of these are logic ties, and most schedules don't model them. Forty days of float in front of a window that closes in forty-one days is one day of float. The software won't say so. Miss the window and the delay is a season, not a week. Put the close dates on the board and check them every morning.","u":"/all.html#the-calendar-fights-back"},{"k":"Field note","t":"Some Resources Are Specific People","x":"The schedule says two Class B welders. The job needs Dave, the only one qualified on that alloy, and Dave is at his daughter's wedding until Tuesday. The histogram shows welders at sixty percent all month, which is true and useless. Name people where one person is the constraint: rare qualifications, signing authority, the operator for a tandem lift. Put their calendars in the plan, and the wedding shows up in March, when it's easy to work around.","u":"/all.html#the-welder-is-on-vacation"},{"k":"Field note","t":"Capture Field Notes Once, in a Usable Shape","x":"Foremen write down what happened every day. Then the same facts get retyped into the schedule update, the timesheet, an RFI, a QC record and, years later, a claim. Capture each item once with four fields: what it blocks, what clears it, who owns it, when it expires. The other documents can then be generated from it. Claims teams already rebuild those four fields from diaries, years later, at legal rates.","u":"/all.html#write-the-story-once"},{"k":"Field note","t":"Use One Breakdown Structure","x":"The estimate, schedule, cost report and work packages each have a breakdown structure. They match on sanction day. By month six there are four versions and a standing meeting to reconcile them. Each group needs a different view: cost by contract, the field by area, the schedule by system. Those can all be views of one structure. One breakdown, with every rollup calculated from it. Copies drift. References don't.","u":"/all.html#one-spine"},{"k":"Field note","t":"Put a Name on Every Green Status","x":"Anyone can mark a status green, so reports drift optimistic. Often green just means nobody wanted the argument. Quality solved this long ago: a weld is accepted because a named inspector stamps it. Do the same for status. A green needs a name next to it. You'll get fewer greens, and you'll be able to believe them.","u":"/all.html#green-costs-a-name"},{"k":"Field note","t":"Measure How Certain the Forecast Is","x":"SPI and CPI measure work done against the plan. Nothing measures how well we know the finish date, which is what the owner is paying for. A job can report green every month while it gets later. Track the range At any status date the finish is a range, say P10 to P90. Settling a question that had spread turnover across eight months can be worth more than a record month of quantities. Plan how fast the range should narrow, then track it. SCI (schedule certainty index) = planned range width ÷ actual range width. Above 1 is better than planned. CCI (cost certainty index) = the same for the estimate at completion. The worrying case is quantities moving while the range stays wide. Scoring the ranges Left alone, everyone reports a narrow range. So score reported ranges against what happened, the way weather forecasters are scored, and penalize overconfidence more than admitted doubt. Most big jobs already run a schedule risk analysis and then discard the distribution. Keeping it is a cheap experiment.","u":"/all.html#sci-cci"},{"k":"Bootcamp primer","t":"STKY and the energy wheel","x":"Most injury programs count everything the same. Energy-based safety asks one question first: is there enough energy here to kill someone? If so, it needs a direct control. The energy wheel lists ten forms of energy: gravity, motion, mechanical, electrical, pressure, temperature, chemical, biological, radiation and sound. Walking the wheel for each task finds far more hazards than walking the job by feel. STKY (the stuff that kills you): high-energy hazards. Research by the Construction Safety Research Alliance put the threshold for a likely serious injury or death at about 1,500 joules, roughly a person falling two metres. Typical STKY: suspended loads, falls from height, mobile equipment, trench walls, energized electrical work, stored pressure, hazardous atmospheres, and radiation sources. A direct control acts on the energy itself, works when installed and used properly, and still protects if someone makes an unintentional mistake: guardrails, lockout, exclusion zones, trench boxes, barricades at a calculated boundary. Indirect controls help but rely on people getting it right every time: training, signs, pre-job briefs, PPE on its own, and 'be careful'. Rank hazards by energy, not by how often they happen. A paper cut happens daily; a dropped load happens once. A clean injury log doesn't mean the STKY is controlled. Ask, for each high-energy hazard: what's the direct control, and is it in place right now?","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#learn=stky-and-the-energy-wheel"},{"k":"Bootcamp primer","t":"INPO and WANO in one page","x":"The nuclear industry polices itself on top of the regulator. INPO (US, founded 1979 after Three Mile Island) and WANO (worldwide, founded 1989 after Chernobyl) set excellence standards, send peers to review plants, and spread lessons from every event. Neither is a regulator. They have no legal power: their pull comes from peer pressure, confidential ratings, and the shared belief that an accident anywhere hurts everyone. Peer reviews: a team from other operators spends about two weeks at a plant, measures it against performance objectives and criteria, and leaves areas for improvement. New units get a pre-startup review before first fuel load. Operating experience: serious events become reports (SOERs) with recommendations every member is expected to act on. Read the new-build lessons before you start construction. Safety culture traits include a questioning attitude, personal accountability, safety communication, leadership values, an environment for raising concerns, continuous learning, and problem identification and resolution. Human performance tools: pre-job briefs, self-checking (STAR: stop, think, act, review), peer checks, three-way communication, procedure use and adherence, place-keeping, and stopping when unsure. Error precursors fall into task demands, individual capabilities, work environment and human nature. Spot them before the job, then set defences. Expect the founding events, 'is it a regulator?' (no), what a peer review is, and which behaviour fits a healthy safety culture or a human performance tool. Don't confuse INPO or WANO with the NRC, the CNSC or the IAEA. Blaming the person who made the error is rarely the answer: ask why the process let it happen.","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#learn=inpo-and-wano-in-one-page"},{"k":"Bootcamp primer","t":"The Agile Manifesto","x":"Written in 2001 by seventeen software developers at Snowbird, Utah. It's four value statements and twelve principles about building things in short, working steps with the customer close by. Four values, each preferring the left over the right without throwing the right away: people and how they work together over processes and tools; working product over exhaustive documentation; collaborating with the customer over negotiating contracts; responding to change over following a fixed plan. The principles stress early and continuous delivery, welcoming changing requirements, delivering every few weeks, business and builders working together daily, motivated people with support and trust, face-to-face talk, working product as the measure of progress, a sustainable pace, technical excellence, simplicity (maximizing the work not done), self-organizing teams, and regular reflection to improve. It's a mindset, not a method: Scrum, Kanban and XP are ways of putting it into practice. Exam questions reward servant leadership, team empowerment, customer collaboration and adapting to change. When an answer imposes a plan, controls the team or protects documentation for its own sake, it's usually wrong. 'Over' doesn't mean 'instead of': plans, documents and contracts still matter. Agile isn't 'no planning'; it's planning often, in smaller pieces.","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#learn=the-agile-manifesto"},{"k":"Bootcamp primer","t":"Earned value management","x":"Earned value tells you, in money, how much work you've actually done compared with what you planned and what you've spent, so you can see trouble early and forecast the finish. PV (planned value): the budgeted cost of the work scheduled to date. EV (earned value): the budgeted cost of the work actually done. AC (actual cost): what that work really cost. Variances: CV = EV − AC (negative is over budget); SV = EV − PV (negative is behind). Indices: CPI = EV ÷ AC and SPI = EV ÷ PV; under 1 is bad, over 1 is good. Forecasts: EAC = BAC ÷ CPI if the trend continues; EAC = AC + (BAC − EV) if the rest goes to plan. ETC = EAC − AC. VAC = BAC − EAC. TCPI = (BAC − EV) ÷ (BAC − AC): the efficiency you need from now on to finish on budget. Expect calculations and interpretation: 'CPI is 0.8: what does it mean and what do you do?' Read which formula the question's assumptions point to. SPI drifts back to 1 at the end of any project, even a late one, because all the planned work is eventually earned. Don't read percent complete or money spent as progress on their own.","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#learn=earned-value-management"},{"k":"Bootcamp primer","t":"The critical path method","x":"The critical path is the longest chain of dependent work through the schedule. It sets the earliest finish: delay anything on it and the project is late. Forward pass: early start and early finish for each activity, from the start. Backward pass: late finish and late start, back from the end. Total float = LS − ES (or LF − EF): how long an activity can slip without moving the finish. Critical activities have zero float (or the least). Free float: how long it can slip without delaying the next activity. Compressing: crashing adds resources to critical work (costs money); fast-tracking overlaps work (adds rework risk). Only critical-path work shortens the project. Near-critical paths matter: shorten the critical path and another one may take over. Questions ask for float, the critical path, or which activity to crash first: pick the critical one with the lowest cost per day saved. Negative float means a deadline can't be met as planned. Shortening non-critical work never brings the finish date in.","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#learn=the-critical-path-method"},{"k":"Bootcamp primer","t":"Risk management","x":"A risk is an uncertain event that would affect the project if it happened: threats hurt it, opportunities help. You plan for them before they happen. Identify, then analyze: qualitative (probability and impact, a heat map) then quantitative (EMV, Monte Carlo, sensitivity). EMV = probability × impact, summed: threats negative, opportunities positive. Threat responses: avoid, transfer (insurance, bonds, contracts), mitigate, accept (actively with contingency, or passively). Opportunities: exploit, share, enhance, accept. Either: escalate when it's beyond the project. Each risk gets an owner, the person best placed to watch and act, and triggers that warn it's coming. Residual risk is what's left after a response; secondary risk is a new risk the response creates. When a risk happens, implement the planned response first. When a new risk appears, record and analyze it. 'Escalate' fits only when it's outside the project's authority. An issue is a risk that has happened. Contingency reserve covers known risks; management reserve covers unknown ones and needs approval to use.","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#learn=risk-management"},{"k":"Bootcamp primer","t":"Integrated change control","x":"Every change to scope, schedule, cost or the baselines goes through one process: request, analyze impact, decide, then update the plans and tell people. Steps: log the change request; assess impact on scope, schedule, cost, quality, risk and resources; take it to the decision-maker (often a change control board); if approved, update baselines and plans and communicate. Scope creep: changes that slip in without control. Gold plating: the team adding extras nobody asked for. Both are wrong even if the customer would like them. In agile, change is welcome but goes through the product owner and the backlog, not into the middle of a sprint. The first step is almost always to evaluate the impact, not to implement, refuse or escalate. After approval: update the baseline, then communicate. Senior stakeholders don't get to skip the process. A 'small' change is still a change.","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#learn=integrated-change-control"},{"k":"Bootcamp primer","t":"Stakeholder engagement","x":"Stakeholders are anyone who affects or is affected by the project. The job is to find them early, understand them and engage each the right way. Identify them early and keep a stakeholder register: interests, influence, expectations, current and desired engagement. Power-interest grid: manage closely (high power, high interest), keep satisfied (high power, low), keep informed (low power, high), monitor (low, low). Engagement levels run from unaware and resistant through neutral to supportive and leading. Communicate in the way each needs: a one-page dashboard for an executive, a toolbox talk for a crew. Answers that listen, meet in person and involve people beat those that ignore, avoid, or go over someone's head. A new stakeholder: update the register and meet them. Don't wait until resistance appears, and don't send everyone everything.","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#learn=stakeholder-engagement"},{"k":"Bootcamp primer","t":"Conflict management","x":"Conflict on projects is normal. Most of it comes from schedules, priorities and resources, not personalities. How you handle it decides whether it helps or hurts. Five approaches: collaborate (problem-solve for a solution both support), compromise (each gives something), smooth (play up agreement), force (decide using authority), withdraw (step back). Collaboration is usually best when there's time and both sides matter. Forcing fits emergencies and safety. Compromise fits when a tolerance band allows splitting it. Address it early and privately where possible; let the people involved try first, and facilitate if they can't. PMI favours collaboration and direct, private conversation. Safety and urgent decisions are the classic exception, where forcing is right. Smoothing and withdrawing buy calm but solve nothing. Escalating straight to HR or managers skips the step where you try.","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#learn=conflict-management"},{"k":"Bootcamp primer","t":"Team development (Tuckman)","x":"Teams go through stages: forming, storming, norming, performing and, at the end, adjourning. Leaders change their style as the team moves through them. Forming: polite, uncertain; give direction. Storming: conflict over how to work; facilitate and set ground rules. Norming: agreement on how they work; support. Performing: self-managing; delegate. Adjourning: wrap up and recognize. Teams can slip back a stage when people join or leave. A team charter, written by the team, sets values, ground rules and how decisions get made. Match the stage to the behaviour in the question, then pick the matching leadership move. Don't impose rules the team should write, and don't treat storming as failure.","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#learn=team-development-tuckman"},{"k":"Bootcamp primer","t":"Leadership and servant leadership","x":"Modern project leadership is mostly about enabling people: removing obstacles, growing them, and earning influence rather than relying on authority. Servant leadership: put the team's needs first, clear impediments, protect them from distraction, help them grow. Situational leadership: directing, coaching, supporting, delegating, depending on the person's skill and commitment. Emotional intelligence: knowing and managing your own emotions and reading others'. Power: expert and referent power (respect) work best; legitimate, reward and coercive power have limits. Motivation: achievement, recognition, growth and the work itself motivate; pay and conditions mostly prevent dissatisfaction. In weak-matrix or agile settings, influence and negotiation beat authority. Private coaching beats public correction. Making all the decisions to 'protect' the team isn't servant leadership.","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#learn=leadership-and-servant-leadership"},{"k":"Bootcamp primer","t":"Contracts and procurement","x":"The contract type decides who carries cost risk. Procurement is choosing sellers fairly and managing them to the contract. Fixed price (firm fixed, incentive fee, economic price adjustment): the seller carries most cost risk; it needs clear scope. Cost-reimbursable (cost plus fixed fee, incentive fee, award fee, percentage of cost): the buyer carries most risk; it suits uncertain scope. Cost plus percentage of cost is the worst for the buyer. Time and materials: rates for labour and materials; for small or unclear work, ideally with a not-to-exceed limit. Point of total assumption (fixed price incentive): above it, the seller bears every extra dollar. Make-or-buy, then RFI (information), RFQ (price) or RFP (solution and approach); select on weighted criteria, not price alone. Claims are best settled by negotiation under the contract's change and dispute clauses. Only the procurement lead talks to bidders during a tender. A verbal go-ahead isn't a change order. Closing a project includes closing its contracts.","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#learn=contracts-and-procurement"},{"k":"Bootcamp primer","t":"Quality management","x":"Quality is meeting requirements and fitness for use. Prevention costs less than finding and fixing defects. Quality assurance looks at the process; quality control inspects the product. Cost of conformance (training, prevention, inspection) versus cost of non-conformance (rework, scrap, warranty). Tools: cause-and-effect (Ishikawa) diagrams for root causes; control charts for process stability (seven points on one side of the mean is a signal, even inside the limits); Pareto charts for the vital few; histograms and check sheets. Hold points stop work until inspected; witness points let work go on if the inspector doesn't attend. Validate scope is the customer formally accepting deliverables that QC has already checked. Continuous improvement (plan, do, check, act) runs throughout. Inspecting quality in at the end is the expensive way. Specification limits are the customer's; control limits are the process's.","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#learn=quality-management"},{"k":"Bootcamp primer","t":"The work breakdown structure","x":"The WBS breaks the project's deliverables into smaller pieces until each can be estimated, scheduled and owned. It's organized around deliverables, not departments or phases of activity. The lowest level is the work package; activities come later, when you schedule. The 100% rule: the WBS covers all the scope, and nothing outside it. The WBS dictionary describes each element; together with the scope statement it forms the scope baseline. 'What's the lowest level of the WBS?' Work package. 'Where's the scope baseline?' Scope statement, WBS and WBS dictionary. Don't organize it by who does the work: that's the organizational breakdown structure (OBS).","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#learn=the-work-breakdown-structure"},{"k":"Bootcamp primer","t":"Scrum in one page","x":"Scrum runs the work in fixed sprints of up to a month, with three roles, a handful of events and a few artefacts, to deliver a usable increment every sprint. Roles: the product owner orders the backlog and owns value; developers build; the scrum master coaches and removes impediments. Events: sprint planning, the daily scrum (fifteen minutes, for the team), the sprint review (inspect the increment with stakeholders), and the retrospective (improve how the team works). Artefacts: product backlog, sprint backlog, increment. The definition of done applies to every item. Velocity is a team's own planning tool, not a performance score; never compare teams by it. New requests go to the product owner, not into the current sprint. The scrum master serves; they don't assign work. A story that passes its acceptance criteria but not the definition of done isn't done.","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#learn=scrum-in-one-page"},{"k":"Bootcamp primer","t":"Kanban and lean flow","x":"Kanban makes work visible, limits work in progress and improves flow. Lean removes waste: anything that doesn't add value for the customer. Visualize the workflow on a board, limit WIP per column, manage flow, make policies explicit, and improve together. Little's law: lead time = work in progress ÷ throughput, so less WIP means faster delivery. A cumulative flow diagram shows a bottleneck as a band that widens. Lean's wastes include waiting, rework, overproduction, excess inventory, extra processing, motion and transport. On site, waiting for materials, drawings and permits is the big one. The Last Planner System has the people doing the work commit to what can really be done next week. When a column overflows, lower the WIP limit and swarm the bottleneck. Starting more work makes it worse. Busy isn't the same as productive.","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#learn=kanban-and-lean-flow"},{"k":"Bootcamp primer","t":"Advanced work packaging","x":"AWP plans the job backwards from the crews: engineering and procurement deliver in the order construction will build, and a crew only gets work with every constraint cleared. Construction work areas, then construction work packages (CWPs), then engineering (EWPs) and procurement packages to feed them. Workface planners turn CWPs into installation work packages (IWPs): a week or two of work for one crew, with drawings, materials, scaffold, tools and permits all in hand. Only constraint-free IWPs are released. Productivity rises mainly because crews stop waiting. It comes from the Construction Industry Institute (CII), not PMI. If an IWP has an open constraint, hold it and release a ready one instead. Starting a package that isn't ready is the waste AWP exists to stop.","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#learn=advanced-work-packaging"},{"k":"Bootcamp primer","t":"BIM in one page","x":"Building information modelling is a shared, data-rich model of the asset used through design, construction and operation. Each discipline keeps its own model; they're combined (federated) for coordination and clash detection. Dimensions: 3D geometry, 4D adds time (the schedule), 5D adds cost. Later 'D's (energy, operations) vary by who you ask. LOD (Level of Development) runs 100 (concept) to 500 (as-built); 400 is fabrication-ready. IFC is the open, vendor-neutral exchange format (ISO 16739); ISO 19650 sets information management and the common data environment. A digital twin links the model to live operating data. Clash detection pays off before construction; a common data environment gives one controlled source of truth. The model doesn't replace issued drawings or the people who coordinate it.","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#learn=bim-in-one-page"},{"k":"Bootcamp primer","t":"GIS in one page","x":"A geographic information system stores data with a location, so you can map it, measure it and ask spatial questions. Vector data: points, lines and polygons (wells, pipelines, parcels). Raster data: a grid of cells (elevation, imagery). Coordinate systems and datums matter: NAD27 against NAD83 can shift data by tens of metres. Reproject everything to one system. Common operations: buffer (everything within a distance), overlay (where layers intersect), spatial select. RTK GPS gets centimetre accuracy with a base station; a phone gets metres. When two surveys don't line up by tens of metres, suspect the datum first. Units scale data; datums shift it.","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#learn=gis-in-one-page"},{"k":"Bootcamp primer","t":"Nuclear quality assurance","x":"Nuclear QA programmes make sure everything important to safety is designed, bought, built and checked under strict control, with records to prove it. Canada: CSA N286 sets management system requirements. US: 10 CFR 50 Appendix B (18 criteria), usually met through ASME NQA-1. A graded approach: the more safety-significant the item, the more rigorous the controls. Commercial grade dedication qualifies an off-the-shelf item for safety-related use by verifying its critical characteristics. Suspect, counterfeit or fraudulent items are quarantined and reported, never used on a guess. Nonconformances are dispositioned as use-as-is, repair, rework or reject, with corrective action aimed at the root cause. Anything measured with an out-of-calibration tool since its last good check is suspect and must be evaluated. Retesting until something passes isn't verification.","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#learn=nuclear-quality-assurance"},{"k":"Bootcamp primer","t":"Ontario's Green Book (OHSA and O. Reg. 213/91)","x":"Ontario's Occupational Health and Safety Act and its Construction Projects regulation set out who is responsible for what on a site, and the minimum rules for the work. Workers' three rights: to know about hazards, to participate in health and safety, and to refuse unsafe work. The constructor has the overall duty to make sure the Act and regulations are followed on the project. Supervisors must be competent: qualified, trained, experienced and aware of the Act and the hazards. Fall protection generally above 3 m (and in set cases lower). Working at heights training is required before using fall protection on construction projects. Trenches more than 1.2 m deep generally need support, sloping or a trench box. A notice of project is required when the project's total cost exceeds $50,000, among other triggers. A joint health and safety committee is needed where 20 or more workers are regularly employed; 6 or more need a health and safety representative. When in doubt, the safe and compliant answer wins over the schedule, every time. These are summaries: the regulation has exceptions and changes. Check the current text on ontario.ca before relying on a number.","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#learn=ontario-s-green-book-ohsa-and-o-reg-213-91"},{"k":"Bootcamp primer","t":"Systems engineering in one page","x":"Systems engineering looks at the whole: what's needed, how the parts fit, and proof that the finished system does the job. The V-model: down the left side, needs become requirements, then design; up the right, parts are integrated and tested against each level. Verification: did we build it right (against the spec)? Validation: did we build the right thing (against the need)? Good requirements are clear, testable, needed and traceable; a traceability matrix links each to design and test. Interface control documents define where systems meet, so both sides build to the same boundary. Trade studies compare options against weighted criteria; single points of failure get redundancy where the stakes demand it. Untraceable requirements and undefined interfaces are where integration problems come from. Two scopes can each assume the other handles the interface.","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#learn=systems-engineering-in-one-page"},{"k":"Bootcamp primer","t":"Why giga projects fail (and some don't)","x":"Bent Flyvbjerg's research on thousands of big projects found they mostly come in over budget, late and under benefits, over and over: his 'iron law'. About 8.5% of projects hit both budget and schedule; about 0.5% hit budget, schedule and the promised benefits. Causes: optimism bias and, sometimes, strategic misrepresentation to win approval. Reference class forecasting fixes estimates by looking at how similar past projects actually turned out. 'Think slow, act fast': plan thoroughly while change is cheap, then deliver quickly, since delivery is when things go wrong. Modular, repeatable designs (solar and wind farms) do best; bespoke one-offs carry the most risk. Use the outside view (similar past projects), not just the team's own estimate. A big budget and a long plan don't make a project safe; untested, unique designs make it fragile.","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#learn=why-giga-projects-fail-and-some-don-t"},{"k":"Bootcamp primer","t":"The PMI Code of Ethics","x":"PMI's code rests on four values: responsibility, respect, fairness and honesty, with standards members aspire to and standards they must meet. Responsibility: own your decisions, keep commitments, report unethical conduct. Respect: listen, deal with conflict directly, don't abuse power. Fairness: be impartial, disclose conflicts of interest, don't favour friends. Honesty: tell the truth about status and qualifications, never mislead. Disclose conflicts of interest and step out of the decision. Decline gifts or follow the gift policy. Report real status even under pressure. Report code violations to PMI. Staying quiet to keep the peace is usually the wrong answer.","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#learn=the-pmi-code-of-ethics"},{"k":"Bootcamp primer","t":"Programs and portfolios","x":"A program is a group of related projects managed together for benefits they couldn't deliver apart. A portfolio is the set of work an organization chooses, balanced against its strategy. Program managers manage interdependencies, shared resources and benefits across projects. Benefits are realized after delivery, usually by benefits owners in the business, as set out in a benefits management plan. Portfolio managers select, rank and balance work by strategic fit, value, risk and capacity, and stop work that no longer fits. Governance bodies steer direction and decide major changes; they don't approve every task. Program questions reward the whole-program view over any one project. Portfolio questions reward ranking, capacity and strategy over enthusiasm or seniority. Delivering outputs isn't realizing benefits. If everything is 'priority one', nothing is.","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#learn=programs-and-portfolios"},{"k":"Bootcamp primer","t":"Estimating","x":"Estimates are forecasts with uncertainty, so say how sure you are. Analogous: from a similar past project (quick, rough). Parametric: a rate times a quantity (for example dollars per cubic metre). Bottom-up: summing the detail (slow, accurate). Three-point: PERT (beta) = (O + 4M + P) ÷ 6, with σ ≈ (P − O) ÷ 6. Triangular = (O + M + P) ÷ 3. Rolling wave: detail the near term and keep later work at a higher level until it's clearer. Ranges and confidence (P50, P80) beat single numbers for anything uncertain. Pick the technique the question's information supports: a past project means analogous; a unit rate means parametric. Padding every estimate hides risk instead of managing it.","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#learn=estimating"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Dana is about to formally launch Northshore. What is the main purpose of the project charter?","x":"Dana is about to formally launch Northshore. What is the main purpose of the project charter? Answer: To authorize the project and give the PM authority. The charter authorizes the project and the PM, and records the high-level objectives, constraints and sponsor. (Project charter, The business case)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=1"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Northshore's budget includes reserves. Which reserve covers unknown-unknowns and is usually controlled by…","x":"Northshore's budget includes reserves. Which reserve covers unknown-unknowns and is usually controlled by management? Answer: Management reserve. Management reserve is outside the cost baseline and covers unforeseen work within scope. Using it requires management approval. (Contingency (budget), The business case)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=2"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Two options for Northshore's cooling system: Option A has an NPV of $120M over 60 years; Option B has an…","x":"Two options for Northshore's cooling system: Option A has an NPV of $120M over 60 years; Option B has an NPV of $95M over 40 years. Which should be chosen on financial grounds? Answer: Option A, because NPV already counts the time value of money. NPV already discounts cash flows to today's dollars. Pick the higher NPV; the project lengths are already built in. (Net present value, The business case)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=3"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Northshore's business case shows an IRR of 6.5%. The province's hurdle rate is 5%. What does this mean?","x":"Northshore's business case shows an IRR of 6.5%. The province's hurdle rate is 5%. What does this mean? Answer: It returns more than the required rate, so it is acceptable. An IRR above the hurdle rate means a positive NPV at that discount rate. (Internal rate of return, The business case)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=4"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"A board member wants Northshore judged by payback period alone. What is the main weakness of payback period…","x":"A board member wants Northshore judged by payback period alone. What is the main weakness of payback period as a selection method? Answer: It ignores cash after payback and the time value of money. Payback is simple but blind to long-term value. For a 60-year nuclear asset, that's a serious flaw. (Payback period, The business case)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=5"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"The province can fund either Northshore (NPV $2B) or a transmission upgrade (NPV $1.5B), not both. If it…","x":"The province can fund either Northshore (NPV $2B) or a transmission upgrade (NPV $1.5B), not both. If it picks Northshore, what is the opportunity cost? Answer: $1.5B. Opportunity cost is the value of the best option you give up: $1.5B. (Opportunity cost, The business case)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=6"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Northshore has spent $800M on an early design that is now obsolete. A new design is cheaper to finish. How…","x":"Northshore has spent $800M on an early design that is now obsolete. A new design is cheaper to finish. How should the $800M affect the decision? Answer: It shouldn't; sunk costs don't count in future decisions. Sunk costs are gone either way. Decide on future costs and benefits only. (Sunk cost, The business case)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=7"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Dana asks who should own Northshore's business case. Who is typically responsible for it?","x":"Dana asks who should own Northshore's business case. Who is typically responsible for it? Answer: The sponsor, often with the PM's input. The sponsor owns the business case and the benefits. The PM may help develop it and keeps it in view during delivery. (Business case, The business case)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=8"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Northshore's benefits (clean baseload power, jobs and lower system emissions) will mostly appear after…","x":"Northshore's benefits (clean baseload power, jobs and lower system emissions) will mostly appear after handover. Who ensures they are realized? Answer: The benefits owner in the business, as the benefits plan sets out. The benefits management plan names owners, metrics and timing, often extending beyond project closure. (Benefits realisation management, The business case)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=9"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"The utility board asks how Northshore's success will be judged. In PMI's 2026 view, what is the main…","x":"The utility board asks how Northshore's success will be judged. In PMI's 2026 view, what is the main measure of project success? Answer: Delivering the intended value and outcomes. The current outline focuses on value and outcomes, not only the triple constraint. (Value (economics), The business case)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=10"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Northshore must pass formal phase gates before major commitments. What is the purpose of these gates?","x":"Northshore must pass formal phase gates before major commitments. What is the purpose of these gates? Answer: To let governance continue, change or stop the project on its merits. Gate reviews are governance decision points: continue, adjust or stop. (Project governance, The business case)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=11"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"The Northshore PMO provides templates and training but has little control over projects. This type of PMO is:","x":"The Northshore PMO provides templates and training but has little control over projects. This type of PMO is: Answer: Supportive. Supportive PMOs act as a resource hub with low control. Controlling PMOs require compliance; directive PMOs manage projects directly. (Project management office, The business case)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=12"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Northshore's four reactor units, the transmission connection and the training centre are managed together…","x":"Northshore's four reactor units, the transmission connection and the training centre are managed together for combined benefits. This is a: Answer: Program. A program is related projects managed together to deliver benefits that wouldn't come from managing them separately. (Program management, The business case)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=13"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"The utility's executive committee ranks Northshore against refurbishments and hydro upgrades by strategic…","x":"The utility's executive committee ranks Northshore against refurbishments and hydro upgrades by strategic fit. This is: Answer: Portfolio management. Portfolio management selects and prioritizes work against the organization's strategy. (Project portfolio management, The business case)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=14"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"The board wants a few measures that show whether Northshore is delivering its business value. Which is most…","x":"The board wants a few measures that show whether Northshore is delivering its business value. Which is most appropriate? Answer: Outcome KPIs tied to the business case, such as forecast cost to complete. Good KPIs measure progress toward outcomes, not activity. (Key performance indicator, The business case)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=15"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Leadership sets an objective, 'Northshore is the safest nuclear build in Canada', with key results such as…","x":"Leadership sets an objective, 'Northshore is the safest nuclear build in Canada', with key results such as a lost-time incident rate below 0.1. This framework is: Answer: OKRs (objectives and key results). OKRs pair a qualitative objective with measurable key results. (OKR, The business case)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=16"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Early on, the team assesses political, economic, social, technological, legal and environmental factors…","x":"Early on, the team assesses political, economic, social, technological, legal and environmental factors affecting Northshore. This is: Answer: PESTLE analysis. PESTLE scans the external environment, which is core to the Business Environment domain. (PEST analysis, The business case)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=17"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Which part of a SWOT for Northshore would 'Ontario's experienced nuclear supply chain' belong in?","x":"Which part of a SWOT for Northshore would 'Ontario's experienced nuclear supply chain' belong in? Answer: Strength. An internal or organizational advantage you control or have is a strength. (SWOT analysis, The business case)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=18"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Northshore's escalation rules say the PM can approve changes up to $5M; above that, the steering committee…","x":"Northshore's escalation rules say the PM can approve changes up to $5M; above that, the steering committee decides. A $7M change is needed urgently. What should you do? Answer: Prepare the impact analysis and take it to the steering committee. Governance thresholds define decision authority. Work within them; use the fast lane if one exists. (Project governance, The business case)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=19"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"The utility considers setting up Northshore as its own division. In which structure does the PM have the…","x":"The utility considers setting up Northshore as its own division. In which structure does the PM have the most authority? Answer: Projectized. In a projectized structure the PM has full authority and the team reports to the PM. (Organizational structure, The business case)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=20"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Your community relations lead comes out of a tense meeting with local residents visibly upset, and snaps at…","x":"Your community relations lead comes out of a tense meeting with local residents visibly upset, and snaps at a colleague. What should you do first? Answer: Talk with them privately and listen to what happened. Emotional intelligence starts with empathy and private, active listening. Understand before you act. (Emotional intelligence, Licensing and the community)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=21"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"At a Northshore community open house, a resident loudly complains that the project is ignoring their…","x":"At a Northshore community open house, a resident loudly complains that the project is ignoring their concerns about truck traffic. What should you do? Answer: Listen, paraphrase their concerns back, and agree a follow-up. Active listening defuses emotion and shows respect. Confirm understanding, then follow up with action. (Active listening, Licensing and the community)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=22"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"The host municipality's mayor, who has high power and high interest, is resisting Northshore's proposed…","x":"The host municipality's mayor, who has high power and high interest, is resisting Northshore's proposed heavy-haul route. How should you engage them? Answer: Manage them closely: meet, understand their concerns and involve them. On the power/interest grid, high-high stakeholders are managed closely. Understand the resistance and engage directly. (Stakeholder analysis, Licensing and the community)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=23"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Halfway through licensing, the regulator assigns a new senior project officer to Northshore. What should…","x":"Halfway through licensing, the regulator assigns a new senior project officer to Northshore. What should you do first? Answer: Update the stakeholder register and meet them to learn their needs. Identify and analyze them first. Register, assess, meet, then tailor engagement and communication. (Stakeholder management, Licensing and the community)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=24"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"A team member asks how to handle a difficult municipal planner who keeps rejecting Northshore's traffic…","x":"A team member asks how to handle a difficult municipal planner who keeps rejecting Northshore's traffic plans. What is the best response? Answer: Coach them with questions that help them work out an approach. Coaching builds capability. Guide them to their own solution. (Coaching, Licensing and the community)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=25"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"A cottage association on the Lake Huron shoreline is opposing Northshore's construction near their…","x":"A cottage association on the Lake Huron shoreline is opposing Northshore's construction near their properties. Which approach is best? Answer: Engage early and openly, listen, and look for ways to address concerns. External stakeholders can stop projects. Early, open engagement reduces resistance and finds workable fixes. (Stakeholder engagement, Licensing and the community)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=26"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Mid-construction, the regulator issues a new requirement for seismic qualification of a system already…","x":"Mid-construction, the regulator issues a new requirement for seismic qualification of a system already designed. What should you do first? Answer: Assess its impact and process it through change control. Compliance is mandatory. Analyze the impact on scope, schedule and cost, then manage it through integrated change control. (Canadian Nuclear Safety Commission, Licensing and the community)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=27"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"The Unit 2 schedule is tight and a supervisor suggests skipping a required hold point inspection. What…","x":"The Unit 2 schedule is tight and a supervisor suggests skipping a required hold point inspection. What should you do? Answer: Refuse; hold points are mandatory, so recover time elsewhere. Compliance isn't negotiable for schedule. Find recovery options that don't break requirements. (Regulatory compliance, Licensing and the community)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=28"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Northshore's site is within the traditional territory of several First Nations. What is the best approach?","x":"Northshore's site is within the traditional territory of several First Nations. What is the best approach? Answer: Engage early and meaningfully, and use their input in decisions. Early, respectful, ongoing engagement is both ethical and essential to the project's legitimacy and licence. (Duty to consult, Licensing and the community)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=29"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Northshore's environmental assessment requires mitigation for fish impingement at the cooling intake. How…","x":"Northshore's environmental assessment requires mitigation for fish impingement at the cooling intake. How should the PM treat these commitments? Answer: As requirements with owners, tracked to verification. Environmental commitments are compliance obligations. Track them like any other requirement. (Environmental impact assessment, Licensing and the community)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=30"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"The sponsor asks how Northshore can reduce the embodied carbon of construction. Which action best supports…","x":"The sponsor asks how Northshore can reduce the embodied carbon of construction. Which action best supports this? Answer: Specify lower-carbon concrete where allowed, and track embodied carbon. Sustainability is now an exam theme. Treat it as a measurable project objective, integrated into the design and procurement. (Sustainability, Licensing and the community)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=31"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Investors ask for Northshore's ESG reporting. Which best describes the PM's role?","x":"Investors ask for Northshore's ESG reporting. Which best describes the PM's role? Answer: Supply accurate project data for the ESG metrics the company reports. PMs supply reliable data and integrate ESG requirements into delivery. (Environmental, social, and governance, Licensing and the community)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=32"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Northshore will generate thousands of tonnes of excavated rock. Which is the most sustainable approach?","x":"Northshore will generate thousands of tonnes of excavated rock. Which is the most sustainable approach? Answer: Reuse it on site or locally where it meets specifications. Reuse cuts emissions, cost and traffic. It's a circular-economy approach. (Circular economy, Licensing and the community)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=33"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Local businesses want to benefit from Northshore. Which approach best balances value and fairness?","x":"Local businesses want to benefit from Northshore. Which approach best balances value and fairness? Answer: A transparent local procurement and hiring program with clear criteria. Structured community-benefit programs deliver social value while protecting procurement fairness. (Corporate social responsibility, Licensing and the community)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=34"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"A news outlet reports an inaccurate story that Northshore's costs have doubled. What should you do?","x":"A news outlet reports an inaccurate story that Northshore's costs have doubled. What should you do? Answer: Work with communications to correct the record with approved facts. Use the organization's approved communication channels, and correct facts transparently. (Public relations, Licensing and the community)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=35"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Two weeks after Northshore's owner's engineering team forms, Raj (civil lead) and Mei (mechanical lead)…","x":"Two weeks after Northshore's owner's engineering team forms, Raj (civil lead) and Mei (mechanical lead) argue in every meeting about the reactor building embed design approach. Progress is slowing. What should you do? Answer: Recognize the storming stage and facilitate an agreement on how design calls get made. Storming is a normal stage. The PM's job is to coach the team through it, usually by agreeing ground rules and a decision process in the team charter, not by punishing anyone or escalating. (Tuckman's stages of group development, Building the owner's team)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=36"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Northshore's new owner's team (engineers, schedulers and the digital squad) keeps clashing over meeting…","x":"Northshore's new owner's team (engineers, schedulers and the digital squad) keeps clashing over meeting times, response-time expectations and who decides what. What should you do? Answer: Facilitate the team in writing a team charter together. A team charter built by the team itself (values, ground rules, decision process, working agreements) gets ownership and prevents repeat conflicts. (Team charter, Building the owner's team)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=37"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"The site engineering team complains that their laptops are too slow to open the 3D plant model. In…","x":"The site engineering team complains that their laptops are too slow to open the 3D plant model. In Herzberg's theory, the laptops are a: Answer: Hygiene factor. Hygiene factors (tools, pay, conditions) cause dissatisfaction when missing but don't motivate when present. Fix them to remove complaints. (Two-factor theory, Building the owner's team)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=38"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"While setting up Northshore's owner team, Dana (your sponsor) asks where conflict usually comes from.…","x":"While setting up Northshore's owner team, Dana (your sponsor) asks where conflict usually comes from. According to PMI, the most common sources of conflict on projects are: Answer: Schedules, priorities and resources. PMI's classic list ranks schedules, project priorities and resources at the top. Personality is near the bottom. (Conflict management, Building the owner's team)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=39"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"A new graduate engineer joins Northshore. They're enthusiastic but have no nuclear construction experience.…","x":"A new graduate engineer joins Northshore. They're enthusiastic but have no nuclear construction experience. Which leadership style fits best? Answer: Directing (telling). High commitment and low competence calls for directing: clear instructions and close supervision while they build skill. (Situational leadership theory, Building the owner's team)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=40"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"A team member is regularly 10 minutes late to the daily site coordination meeting, even though the team…","x":"A team member is regularly 10 minutes late to the daily site coordination meeting, even though the team charter says meetings start on time. What should you do? Answer: Talk with them privately, referring to the ground rules the team agreed. Ground rules belong to the team, and the PM enforces them respectfully, starting with a private conversation. (Ground rules, Building the owner's team)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=41"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Northshore runs as a weak matrix: your engineers report to functional managers and you have little formal…","x":"Northshore runs as a weak matrix: your engineers report to functional managers and you have little formal authority. Which types of power are most effective? Answer: Expert and referent power. With little formal authority, influence comes from your expertise and from people respecting and wanting to work with you. (French and Raven's bases of power, Building the owner's team)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=42"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Northshore's newly formed owner's team doesn't know each other and is hesitant to share ideas. What should…","x":"Northshore's newly formed owner's team doesn't know each other and is hesitant to share ideas. What should you do? Answer: Hold team-building activities and set up early chances to collaborate. In the forming stage, the PM helps people get to know each other and build trust. (Team building, Building the owner's team)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=43"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Dana asks what to look for when hiring Northshore's area managers. Which is the core of emotional…","x":"Dana asks what to look for when hiring Northshore's area managers. Which is the core of emotional intelligence for a project leader? Answer: Recognizing and managing your own emotions and those of others. Emotional intelligence covers self-awareness, self-management, social awareness and relationship management. (Emotional intelligence, Building the owner's team)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=44"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"A new owner's team member asks you the difference between leadership and management. Which statement best…","x":"A new owner's team member asks you the difference between leadership and management. Which statement best describes it? Answer: Leadership guides and inspires people; management directs resources and processes. PMI frames leadership as influencing people toward a vision and management as running the system. PMs need both. (Leadership, Building the owner's team)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=45"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Dana asks you what a high-performing Northshore team will look like. Which description fits best?","x":"Dana asks you what a high-performing Northshore team will look like. Which description fits best? Answer: Trust, open communication, shared goals and mutual accountability. High-performing teams have trust, productive conflict, commitment and accountability. (Team, Building the owner's team)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=46"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"You are forming Northshore's integrated owner's team. What is a benefit of a diverse team?","x":"You are forming Northshore's integrated owner's team. What is a benefit of a diverse team? Answer: Broader perspectives that lead to better decisions. Diversity brings different viewpoints. It can raise conflict, but it improves outcomes when managed well. (Diversity (business), Building the owner's team)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=47"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"An owner's team engineer asks to work remotely two days a week. Their design review work can be done…","x":"An owner's team engineer asks to work remotely two days a week. Their design review work can be done remotely. What should you do? Answer: Agree how it will work with the team, within company policy. Within policy, flexibility supports engagement. Make the arrangement transparent so the team can coordinate. (Team, Building the owner's team)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=48"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"A vendor bidding on the turbine contract offers you tickets to a hockey game. What should you do?","x":"A vendor bidding on the turbine contract offers you tickets to a hockey game. What should you do? Answer: Decline, or follow the gift policy and disclose it. PMI's code requires avoiding conflicts of interest. Gifts from bidders during procurement are a clear red flag. (Code of ethics, Building the owner's team)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=49"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"You discover your brother-in-law's firm is bidding on a Northshore contract you're evaluating. What should…","x":"You discover your brother-in-law's firm is bidding on a Northshore contract you're evaluating. What should you do? Answer: Disclose the relationship and step out of the evaluation. Disclose any conflict of interest and step away from the decision. (Conflict of interest, Building the owner's team)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=50"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"You learn that a Northshore colleague falsified the training hours on their PMP application. What should…","x":"You learn that a Northshore colleague falsified the training hours on their PMP application. What should you do? Answer: Report it to PMI, as the code of ethics requires. PMI's code of ethics requires reporting violations to PMI. (Code of ethics, Building the owner's team)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=51"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"A local official hints that permits would move faster with a 'consulting fee'. What should you do?","x":"A local official hints that permits would move faster with a 'consulting fee'. What should you do? Answer: Refuse and report it. Bribery is illegal and unethical. Refuse it and report it. (Bribery, Building the owner's team)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=52"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Your sponsor asks you to report the Unit 1 schedule as 'on track' to the board, although it's two months…","x":"Your sponsor asks you to report the Unit 1 schedule as 'on track' to the board, although it's two months behind. What should you do? Answer: Report the true status and discuss recovery with the sponsor. Honesty is a core value in the code. Give accurate reporting, with options. (Code of ethics, Building the owner's team)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=53"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Northshore's culture is hierarchical and risk-averse. You want to introduce more agile ways of working.…","x":"Northshore's culture is hierarchical and risk-averse. You want to introduce more agile ways of working. What should you consider first? Answer: Adapt the approach to the culture and build support gradually. Organizational culture is an enterprise environmental factor. Tailor the change to it. (Organizational culture, Building the owner's team)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=54"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"If Northshore moved from a weak to a strong matrix, who would usually have more authority over project…","x":"If Northshore moved from a weak to a strong matrix, who would usually have more authority over project resources? Answer: The project manager. In a strong matrix, the PM has moderate to high authority. In a weak matrix, functional managers do. (Matrix management, Building the owner's team)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=55"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Ontario faces a shortage of nuclear-qualified welders while three major nuclear projects run at once. What…","x":"Ontario faces a shortage of nuclear-qualified welders while three major nuclear projects run at once. What is the best approach? Answer: Plan the workforce early: training, apprenticeships, a realistic schedule. External market conditions are a business environment factor. Plan for them strategically, with training pipelines. (Skilled worker, Building the owner's team)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=56"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Northshore's work is covered by building-trades collective agreements. A crew assignment conflicts with…","x":"Northshore's work is covered by building-trades collective agreements. A crew assignment conflicts with jurisdiction rules. What should you do? Answer: Follow the agreements and resolve jurisdiction with labour relations. Labour agreements are legal and contractual constraints. Respect them and resolve disputes through the proper process. (Collective agreement, Building the owner's team)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=57"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"A Northshore worker raises a safety concern about scaffolding but fears retaliation. What should you do?","x":"A Northshore worker raises a safety concern about scaffolding but fears retaliation. What should you do? Answer: Thank them, act on it, and make clear raising safety is protected. A strong nuclear safety culture depends on people feeling free to raise concerns without fear. (Nuclear safety culture, Building the owner's team)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=58"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Northshore will change how the operations organization works (new procedures, roles and tools). Which model…","x":"Northshore will change how the operations organization works (new procedures, roles and tools). Which model helps individuals through the change? Answer: ADKAR. ADKAR guides individual change adoption step by step. (Change management, Building the owner's team)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=59"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"The future operations staff are resisting the new digital work management system. What should you do first?","x":"The future operations staff are resisting the new digital work management system. What should you do first? Answer: Understand their concerns and involve them in the rollout. Resistance often has valid reasons. Engagement turns resisters into co-designers. (Change management, Building the owner's team)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=60"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"For the new station to be ready to operate, what must happen besides construction finishing?","x":"For the new station to be ready to operate, what must happen besides construction finishing? Answer: Operational readiness: trained staff, procedures and systems. Value is realized only when the organization can operate what was built. (Organizational change, Building the owner's team)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=61"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Owen, your lead scheduler, has missed three schedule updates in a row. What is your first step?","x":"Owen, your lead scheduler, has missed three schedule updates in a row. What is your first step? Answer: Meet privately to understand the cause and agree a way forward. PMI's mindset is to understand the root cause first, privately. There may be a skill gap, workload clash or personal issue. (Performance management, Planning the build)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=62"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"A junior project controls analyst wants to grow into a Northshore scheduler, but you have little time to…","x":"A junior project controls analyst wants to grow into a Northshore scheduler, but you have little time to mentor. What is best? Answer: Pair them with an experienced scheduler and agree development goals. Supporting growth through mentoring with clear goals builds capability and engagement. (Mentorship, Planning the build)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=63"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Northshore's project controls team lacks skills in the new integrated scheduling tool, which goes live next…","x":"Northshore's project controls team lacks skills in the new integrated scheduling tool, which goes live next month. What should you do? Answer: Assess the gap, arrange training and put the training time in the schedule. Training is a planned project activity. Identify the gap and plan for it, including the schedule time. (Training and development, Planning the build)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=64"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Dana, your sponsor, criticized the planning team publicly in a steering meeting. Morale has dropped. What…","x":"Dana, your sponsor, criticized the planning team publicly in a steering meeting. Morale has dropped. What should you do? Answer: Meet the sponsor privately to understand their concerns and explain the impact. Raise it privately and respectfully. Understand what's behind the criticism and address both the concern and the impact. (Project sponsor, Planning the build)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=65"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Your engineering team is split 50/50 on two technically sound construction methods for the cooling-water…","x":"Your engineering team is split 50/50 on two technically sound construction methods for the cooling-water intake tunnel and has debated for two meetings. What should you do? Answer: Agree a decision method, such as weighted criteria, and lead them to a decision. Use a structured decision method (multicriteria analysis) to break the deadlock while keeping the decision in the team. (Decision-making, Planning the build)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=66"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"A board member says they're overwhelmed by Northshore's 80-page weekly report and doesn't read it. What…","x":"A board member says they're overwhelmed by Northshore's 80-page weekly report and doesn't read it. What should you do? Answer: Ask what they need and tailor it, for example a one-page dashboard. Communication is tailored to each stakeholder's needs. Update the communications management plan. (Communication plan, Planning the build)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=67"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Northshore's culture tends to hide bad news from executives. The Unit 1 schedule is slipping. What should…","x":"Northshore's culture tends to hide bad news from executives. The Unit 1 schedule is slipping. What should you do? Answer: Report the true status, with recovery options. PMI's code of conduct requires honesty. Bad news early, with options, is the professional standard. (Organizational culture, Planning the build)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=68"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Dana expects Unit 1 in service two months earlier than the approved schedule allows. What should you do?","x":"Dana expects Unit 1 in service two months earlier than the approved schedule allows. What should you do? Answer: Meet the sponsor and present options with their trade-offs. Manage expectations with data and options (fast-track, crash, cut scope), and let the sponsor make an informed trade-off. (Expectation management, Planning the build)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=69"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"In the site preparation schedule, A (clearing, 3 days) leads to B (stripping, 5 days) and C (fencing, 2…","x":"In the site preparation schedule, A (clearing, 3 days) leads to B (stripping, 5 days) and C (fencing, 2 days). Both lead to D (grading, 4 days). What is the duration? Answer: 12 days. The paths are A-B-D = 12 and A-C-D = 9. The longest path, the critical path, is 12 days. (Critical path method, Planning the build)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=70"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"In the turbine hall schedule, an activity has ES = 5, LS = 9 and a duration of 3. What is its total float?","x":"In the turbine hall schedule, an activity has ES = 5, LS = 9 and a duration of 3. What is its total float? Answer: 4 days. Total float = LS − ES = 9 − 5 = 4 days. (Float (project management), Planning the build)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=71"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"For one containment liner ring weld-out: optimistic = 4 days, most likely = 7, pessimistic = 16. What is…","x":"For one containment liner ring weld-out: optimistic = 4 days, most likely = 7, pessimistic = 16. What is the PERT (beta) estimate? Answer: 8 days. (O + 4M + P) / 6 = (4 + 28 + 16) / 6 = 48 / 6 = 8 days. (Program evaluation and review technique, Planning the build)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=72"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Using the same liner ring estimates (O = 4, P = 16), what is the PERT standard deviation?","x":"Using the same liner ring estimates (O = 4, P = 16), what is the PERT standard deviation? Answer: 2 days. σ = (P − O) / 6 = 12 / 6 = 2 days. (Program evaluation and review technique, Planning the build)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=73"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"After levelling boilermaker craft across Units 1 and 2, what is the most likely effect on the schedule?","x":"After levelling boilermaker craft across Units 1 and 2, what is the most likely effect on the schedule? Answer: The project end date may be extended. Levelling adjusts start dates to fit resource constraints, which often extends the schedule and can change the critical path. (Resource leveling, Planning the build)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=74"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"As you build the Northshore WBS, what is its lowest level called?","x":"As you build the Northshore WBS, what is its lowest level called? Answer: Work package. The work package is the lowest WBS level. It's where cost and duration can be reliably estimated and managed. (Work breakdown structure, Planning the build)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=75"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Dana asks whether the Northshore WBS follows the 100% rule. What is the 100% rule?","x":"Dana asks whether the Northshore WBS follows the 100% rule. What is the 100% rule? Answer: The WBS includes all of the project work and only that work. The 100% rule: the WBS captures all deliverables and work, including project management, and nothing outside scope. (Work breakdown structure, Planning the build)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=76"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Dana wants the probability of hitting Northshore's Unit 1 in-service date. Which technique uses many…","x":"Dana wants the probability of hitting Northshore's Unit 1 in-service date. Which technique uses many simulations to produce a probability distribution of completion dates? Answer: Monte Carlo simulation. Monte Carlo runs thousands of iterations with random durations to show the probability of finishing by each date. (Monte Carlo method, Planning the build)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=77"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Northshore's turbine hall roof steel cannot be erected until the columns are grouted and cured. What type…","x":"Northshore's turbine hall roof steel cannot be erected until the columns are grouted and cured. What type of dependency is this? Answer: Mandatory (hard logic). Physical or contractual necessity creates mandatory dependencies. You cannot set roof steel on uncured grout. (Precedence diagramming method, Planning the build)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=78"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"The regulator must issue the construction licence before nuclear-safety-related concrete can be placed.…","x":"The regulator must issue the construction licence before nuclear-safety-related concrete can be placed. This is a(n): Answer: External dependency. The constraint comes from outside the project team's control, so it's external. (Precedence diagramming method, Planning the build)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=79"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Your scheduler lets drywall in the admin building start 3 days before painting prep finishes in each zone,…","x":"Your scheduler lets drywall in the admin building start 3 days before painting prep finishes in each zone, using an FS relationship. This is a: Answer: Lead. A lead pulls the successor earlier (negative lag), allowing overlap. (Lead time, Planning the build)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=80"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Formwork on the reactor building wall can't be stripped until 7 days after the pour. In the schedule this…","x":"Formwork on the reactor building wall can't be stripped until 7 days after the pour. In the schedule this is shown as a: Answer: Finish-to-start with a 7-day lag. A lag is a required delay between activities, such as concrete curing time. (Lag (project management), Planning the build)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=81"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Early in Northshore's business case, you estimate cost using the actual costs of two recent CANDU…","x":"Early in Northshore's business case, you estimate cost using the actual costs of two recent CANDU refurbishments, adjusted for scale. This is: Answer: Analogous estimating. Analogous (top-down) estimating uses similar past projects. It's quick, early and less accurate. (Cost estimate, Planning the build)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=82"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"The civil estimator prices containment concrete at $2,100 per cubic metre placed, times the quantity from…","x":"The civil estimator prices containment concrete at $2,100 per cubic metre placed, times the quantity from the 3D model. This is: Answer: Parametric estimating. Parametric estimating multiplies a unit rate by a quantity. It's accurate when the rate and quantities are reliable. (Cost estimate, Planning the build)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=83"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"For Northshore's definitive (Class 1) estimate, which estimate type is the most accurate and takes the most…","x":"For Northshore's definitive (Class 1) estimate, which estimate type is the most accurate and takes the most effort? Answer: Bottom-up. Bottom-up rolls up detailed work-package estimates. It's the most accurate and time-consuming. (Cost estimate, Planning the build)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=84"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"The cost baseline for Northshore includes:","x":"The cost baseline for Northshore includes: Answer: Work package estimates plus contingency reserves. Cost baseline = estimates + contingency reserve. Add management reserve to get the project budget. (Cost baseline, Planning the build)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=85"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"A cumulative cost chart for Northshore shaped like an S is typically used to:","x":"A cumulative cost chart for Northshore shaped like an S is typically used to: Answer: Compare planned and actual spend with earned value. The S-curve plots cumulative PV, EV and AC, which is the visual heart of earned value reporting. (S-curve, Planning the build)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=86"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Northshore's Unit 4 work is 6 years out and its design isn't finished. How should it be scheduled now?","x":"Northshore's Unit 4 work is 6 years out and its design isn't finished. How should it be scheduled now? Answer: Rolling wave: detail the near term, keep Unit 4 high level for now. Rolling wave planning is progressive elaboration: detail where you have information, placeholders for later work. (Rolling-wave planning, Planning the build)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=87"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"For each commissioning activity, you need to show who is Responsible, Accountable, Consulted and Informed.…","x":"For each commissioning activity, you need to show who is Responsible, Accountable, Consulted and Informed. Which tool? Answer: RACI chart. A RACI is a responsibility assignment matrix. There should be exactly one 'A' per activity. (Responsibility assignment matrix, Planning the build)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=88"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Northshore's core coordination group grows from 10 to 15 people. How many more communication channels are…","x":"Northshore's core coordination group grows from 10 to 15 people. How many more communication channels are there? Answer: 60. n(n−1)/2: 15 people = 105 channels, 10 people = 45. The increase is 60. (Communication, Planning the build)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=89"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"You need to tell the full Northshore workforce (3,000 people) about a new site safety rule. Which…","x":"You need to tell the full Northshore workforce (3,000 people) about a new site safety rule. Which communication method fits best? Answer: Push it through several channels, then confirm at toolbox talks. Large audiences need push, using several channels, with a check for understanding on safety-critical messages. (Communication, Planning the build)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=90"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"A design clash between a cable tray and an HVAC duct is found and must be resolved this week. Where should…","x":"A design clash between a cable tray and an HVAC duct is found and must be resolved this week. Where should it be tracked? Answer: Issue log. It has already happened, so it's an issue, not a risk. Track it in the issue log with an owner and due date. (Issue log, Planning the build)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=91"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"After applying the mitigation for crane-collapse risk, some risk remains. This is called:","x":"After applying the mitigation for crane-collapse risk, some risk remains. This is called: Answer: Residual risk. Residual risk is what remains after the response is implemented. (Risk management, Planning the build)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=92"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Adding a second crane to cut lift risk creates a new risk of the two cranes swinging into each other. This…","x":"Adding a second crane to cut lift risk creates a new risk of the two cranes swinging into each other. This is a: Answer: Secondary risk. Secondary risks arise directly from implementing a risk response. (Risk management, Planning the build)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=93"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Who should own the risk of late delivery of the reactor pressure vessel forging?","x":"Who should own the risk of late delivery of the reactor pressure vessel forging? Answer: The person best placed to watch and respond, such as the supply chain lead. Risk owners are assigned by who can best manage the response. The PM stays accountable overall. (Risk management, Planning the build)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=94"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Which chart shows which risks have the most effect on Northshore's total cost, ranked as horizontal bars?","x":"Which chart shows which risks have the most effect on Northshore's total cost, ranked as horizontal bars? Answer: Tornado diagram. The tornado diagram is the visual from sensitivity analysis: the widest bar is most influential. (Tornado diagram, Planning the build)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=95"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Northshore's plant construction is predictive, but the field app is agile. How should you integrate them?","x":"Northshore's plant construction is predictive, but the field app is agile. How should you integrate them? Answer: Hybrid: agile releases sit in the integrated schedule as milestones. Tailoring means choosing the right life cycle for each part and integrating them at milestones and in governance. (Hybrid project management, Planning the build)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=96"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Your controls team wants to use an AI model to forecast schedule risk. What is a key governance step?","x":"Your controls team wants to use an AI model to forecast schedule risk. What is a key governance step? Answer: Validate its outputs and keep people accountable for decisions. The 2026 outline stresses responsible AI use: transparency, validation and human accountability. (Artificial intelligence, Planning the build)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=97"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"An AI tool used to screen craft job applicants seems to reject candidates from certain regions more often.…","x":"An AI tool used to screen craft job applicants seems to reject candidates from certain regions more often. What should you do? Answer: Stop using it, investigate the bias, and involve HR and legal. Biased AI decisions create ethical and legal risk. Investigate and correct before using it further. (Algorithmic bias, Planning the build)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=98"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"The field app collects worker location data for safety tracking. What must be considered?","x":"The field app collects worker location data for safety tracking. What must be considered? Answer: Privacy law, consent, data minimization and secure handling. Data privacy is a compliance and ethical issue. Collect only what's needed, with consent and protection. (Information privacy, Planning the build)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=99"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"The digital team proposes using AI to auto-generate installation work packages. What's the best first step?","x":"The digital team proposes using AI to auto-generate installation work packages. What's the best first step? Answer: Run a controlled pilot with human review and measure quality. Pilot, validate and scale is a sound way to adopt AI in a high-consequence setting. (Machine learning, Planning the build)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=100"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"A contractor wants to connect their laptop to the plant's construction-phase control system network for…","x":"A contractor wants to connect their laptop to the plant's construction-phase control system network for testing. What should you do? Answer: Follow cybersecurity procedures: approved, scanned devices only. Nuclear cybersecurity requirements are strict. Process protects the plant. (Computer security, Planning the build)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=101"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Northshore's design is split between Toronto, the reactor vendor's office in Europe and an engineering…","x":"Northshore's design is split between Toronto, the reactor vendor's office in Europe and an engineering partner overseas. Design package handoffs keep getting dropped. What is the best action? Answer: Agree team norms on overlap hours, handoff notes and async updates. Distributed teams need explicit working agreements: overlap windows, handoff protocols and communication norms, ideally written up with the team. (Virtual team, Contracts and supply chain)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=102"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"An engineer seconded from the reactor vendor's overseas office never raises issues in design reviews, even…","x":"An engineer seconded from the reactor vendor's overseas office never raises issues in design reviews, even when problems exist. What should you do? Answer: Offer several ways to raise issues and invite their views directly. Adapt to cultural differences by creating safe, varied channels for input. (Cross-cultural communication, Contracts and supply chain)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=103"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"A procurement analyst uses a public AI chatbot to summarize the confidential bids for the reactor coolant…","x":"A procurement analyst uses a public AI chatbot to summarize the confidential bids for the reactor coolant pumps. What should you do? Answer: Stop it, explain the data policy, and point them to approved tools. Confidential data in unapproved tools is a policy and security issue. Correct it, educate, and offer an approved route. (Generative artificial intelligence, Contracts and supply chain)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=104"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"You are overloaded and need to delegate coordination of the Northshore module vendors. Who should get it?","x":"You are overloaded and need to delegate coordination of the Northshore module vendors. Who should get it? Answer: A capable team member who wants to grow, given clear expectations. Delegate to someone capable and motivated, with clear outcomes and matching authority. (Delegation, Contracts and supply chain)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=105"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"The team needs creative ways around a long-lead forging shortage, but quieter members rarely speak up in…","x":"The team needs creative ways around a long-lead forging shortage, but quieter members rarely speak up in brainstorms. Which technique helps most? Answer: Brainwriting or the nominal group technique. Silent idea generation, then sharing and ranking, gives everyone an equal voice. (Brainstorming, Contracts and supply chain)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=106"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Northshore buys cargo insurance for the turbine generator during its ocean shipment. This is which risk…","x":"Northshore buys cargo insurance for the turbine generator during its ocean shipment. This is which risk response? Answer: Transfer. Transfer shifts the impact and ownership to a third party, such as an insurer or contractor. (Risk management, Contracts and supply chain)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=107"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"If the cooling-water intake finishes before ice season, Northshore saves a whole winter of standby costs.…","x":"If the cooling-water intake finishes before ice season, Northshore saves a whole winter of standby costs. You assign your best crew and extra shifts to guarantee it. This response is: Answer: Exploit. Exploit makes sure an opportunity happens: it raises the probability to 100%. (Risk management, Contracts and supply chain)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=108"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Northshore wants to contract the security fence and gatehouse, which has a complete, stable design. Which…","x":"Northshore wants to contract the security fence and gatehouse, which has a complete, stable design. Which contract type fits best? Answer: Firm fixed price. Well-defined scope suits FFP: the seller carries the cost risk and the buyer gets price certainty. (Fixed-price contract, Contracts and supply chain)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=109"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"The reactor vendor's first-of-a-kind module work has highly uncertain scope. Which contract type puts the…","x":"The reactor vendor's first-of-a-kind module work has highly uncertain scope. Which contract type puts the most cost risk on the buyer? Answer: Cost plus percentage of cost. CPPC puts the most risk on the buyer and rewards the seller for spending more. It's often prohibited in public procurement. (Cost-plus contract, Contracts and supply chain)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=110"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"You need a specialty engineering firm for a few weeks of undefined troubleshooting on dewatering. Which…","x":"You need a specialty engineering firm for a few weeks of undefined troubleshooting on dewatering. Which contract fits? Answer: Time and materials with a not-to-exceed limit. T&M suits small, undefined, short work. A not-to-exceed cap controls the buyer's risk. (Time and materials, Contracts and supply chain)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=111"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"A 7-year supply contract for reinforcing steel is exposed to commodity price swings. Which contract type…","x":"A 7-year supply contract for reinforcing steel is exposed to commodity price swings. Which contract type helps? Answer: Fixed price with economic price adjustment. FP-EPA adjusts the price against an agreed index, such as a steel price index, protecting both parties over long durations. (Fixed-price contract, Contracts and supply chain)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=112"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Northshore needs proposals for an innovative approach to marine intake tunnelling where cost is not the…","x":"Northshore needs proposals for an innovative approach to marine intake tunnelling where cost is not the only factor. Which document? Answer: Request for proposal (RFP). An RFP seeks a solution and an approach, evaluated on several criteria. An RFQ is price-focused for defined items. (Request for proposal, Contracts and supply chain)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=113"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"When evaluating bids for the turbine generator, which approach selects on more than price?","x":"When evaluating bids for the turbine generator, which approach selects on more than price? Answer: Weighted source selection criteria. Weighted criteria (technical, experience, schedule, price) produce best-value selection. (Procurement, Contracts and supply chain)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=114"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"During bid evaluation for the cooling-water intake screens, a bidder calls you with new information about…","x":"During bid evaluation for the cooling-water intake screens, a bidder calls you with new information about their price. What should you do? Answer: Decline, and keep all communication with the procurement lead. Fairness in public procurement requires controlled communications. Side channels can void the process. (Procurement, Contracts and supply chain)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=115"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"The excavation contractor submits a claim saying the rock was harder than the geotechnical baseline report…","x":"The excavation contractor submits a claim saying the rock was harder than the geotechnical baseline report showed. What is the preferred way to resolve it? Answer: Negotiation, using the contract's change and dispute clauses. PMI prefers negotiation. Use the contract terms and the geotechnical baseline report as the shared facts; arbitration and litigation are later steps. (Claims, Contracts and supply chain)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=116"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Northshore is deciding whether to fabricate pipe spools in-house or buy from a shop. What analysis should…","x":"Northshore is deciding whether to fabricate pipe spools in-house or buy from a shop. What analysis should come first? Answer: Make-or-buy analysis of cost, capacity, quality and risk. Make-or-buy analysis compares total cost, capability, control and risk before procurement starts. (Make-or-buy decision, Contracts and supply chain)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=117"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"A seller is late delivering embedded plates, and this threatens the Unit 1 pour. The contract has a…","x":"A seller is late delivering embedded plates, and this threatens the Unit 1 pour. The contract has a liquidated damages clause. What should you do first? Answer: Review the contract, notify the seller formally and work on recovery. Administer the contract: confirm the facts, give formal notice per the contract, and focus first on recovering the delivery. (Procurement, Contracts and supply chain)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=118"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Northshore's turbine hall steel contract is fixed price incentive fee. What is the point of total…","x":"Northshore's turbine hall steel contract is fixed price incentive fee. What is the point of total assumption (PTA)? Answer: The cost above which the seller bears all overruns. PTA = (ceiling price − target price) / buyer's share + target cost. Above it, the seller absorbs every dollar. (Point of total assumption, Contracts and supply chain)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=119"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Construction inflation is running well above the escalation assumed in Northshore's estimate. What should…","x":"Construction inflation is running well above the escalation assumed in Northshore's estimate. What should you do? Answer: Reassess the forecast, update the risk register and brief governance. External economic changes must be analyzed and reported transparently. (Inflation, Contracts and supply chain)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=120"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Interest rates rise sharply during Northshore's construction. Why does this matter so much for a nuclear…","x":"Interest rates rise sharply during Northshore's construction. Why does this matter so much for a nuclear project? Answer: Financing during long construction can change total cost and NPV a lot. Long, capital-intensive builds are very sensitive to financing costs. Every month of delay compounds it. (Interest rate, Contracts and supply chain)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=121"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"A single overseas supplier makes a critical reactor component, and geopolitical tension threatens the…","x":"A single overseas supplier makes a critical reactor component, and geopolitical tension threatens the delivery. What should you do? Answer: Assess the risk and plan responses, such as a second source or buffer stock. Geopolitical and supply-chain risks are part of the business environment. Analyze and respond proactively. (Supply chain, Contracts and supply chain)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=122"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"The reactor vendor's design documents are proprietary. Your team wants to share them with a fabricator.…","x":"The reactor vendor's design documents are proprietary. Your team wants to share them with a fabricator. What should you do? Answer: Check the licence terms and get the vendor's approval first. Intellectual property rights are contractual and legal constraints. Follow the licence terms. (Intellectual property, Contracts and supply chain)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=123"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Your team just finished the reactor building excavation two weeks early, through a brutal Lake Huron…","x":"Your team just finished the reactor building excavation two weeks early, through a brutal Lake Huron winter. What is the best way to recognize them? Answer: Recognize the team publicly, and each person in a way that matters to them. Timely recognition, tailored to what each person values, reinforces the behaviour. Do it now, and include the whole team. (Employee recognition, Breaking ground)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=124"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"The excavation team quickly agrees on a risky dewatering approach for the shoreline excavation, and nobody…","x":"The excavation team quickly agrees on a risky dewatering approach for the shoreline excavation, and nobody raises any concerns. What should you do? Answer: Draw out dissent, using a devil's advocate or anonymous input. Fast, unchallenged agreement on something risky is a groupthink warning sign. Build in structured dissent. (Groupthink, Breaking ground)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=125"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"You take over the site preparation package from another PM mid-stream. The team is wary of you. What should…","x":"You take over the site preparation package from another PM mid-stream. The team is wary of you. What should you do first? Answer: Meet each team member to learn their work, concerns and ways of working. Build relationships and understanding first. Trust comes before changing anything. (Interpersonal skills, Breaking ground)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=126"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"A site engineer complains to you about a colleague's behaviour in the field trailer. What should you do first?","x":"A site engineer complains to you about a colleague's behaviour in the field trailer. What should you do first? Answer: Encourage them to talk to the colleague, and offer to facilitate if needed. PMI prefers that the people involved try first, with the PM available as facilitator. (Conflict management, Breaking ground)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=127"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"A field engineer admits a survey layout error that cost two days of excavation. How should you respond?","x":"A field engineer admits a survey layout error that cost two days of excavation. How should you respond? Answer: Thank them for raising it, fix it, and look at the process that allowed it. Rewarding transparency keeps problems surfacing early. Punishing it hides the next one. (Psychological safety, Breaking ground)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=128"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"A site engineer's quality of work has improved a lot after coaching. What should you do?","x":"A site engineer's quality of work has improved a lot after coaching. What should you do? Answer: Give specific, timely positive feedback. Specific, timely feedback reinforces the behaviour. (Feedback, Breaking ground)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=129"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Northshore's site preparation is behind schedule. Dana wants to recover time without adding cost. Which…","x":"Northshore's site preparation is behind schedule. Dana wants to recover time without adding cost. Which technique fits best? Answer: Fast tracking. Fast tracking overlaps activities in parallel. It adds risk and rework potential but usually no direct cost. (Fast tracking, Breaking ground)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=130"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"To crash the site preparation schedule, you should first add resources to which activities?","x":"To crash the site preparation schedule, you should first add resources to which activities? Answer: Critical path activities with the lowest cost per day saved. Crashing targets critical activities, cheapest first, for the time saved. Watch for new critical paths appearing. (Crashing (project management), Breaking ground)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=131"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Geotechnical borings reveal a possible karst zone under the turbine hall footprint. What should you do first?","x":"Geotechnical borings reveal a possible karst zone under the turbine hall footprint. What should you do first? Answer: Record it in the risk register and analyze it. Log it, analyze probability and impact, then plan a response. Escalate only if it's outside your authority or project. (Risk management, Breaking ground)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=132"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"A risk already in the register, a spring storm surge flooding the shoreline excavation, occurs. What should…","x":"A risk already in the register, a spring storm surge flooding the shoreline excavation, occurs. What should you do first? Answer: Implement the planned risk response. When an identified risk happens, run the response you already planned (the contingency plan). Then update records and communicate. (Risk management, Breaking ground)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=133"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"An unidentified risk occurs: the excavation uncovers a buried 1950s fuel tank. What is the correct response?","x":"An unidentified risk occurs: the excavation uncovers a buried 1950s fuel tank. What is the correct response? Answer: Apply a workaround. Unplanned issues get workarounds. Contingency plans exist only for identified risks. (Risk management, Breaking ground)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=134"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"For the shoreline cofferdam, there's a 30% chance of a $50,000 storm repair and a 20% chance of a $20,000…","x":"For the shoreline cofferdam, there's a 30% chance of a $50,000 storm repair and a 20% chance of a $20,000 saving from early dewatering. What is the net EMV? Answer: −$11,000. EMV = (0.3 × −50k) + (0.2 × +20k) = −15k + 4k = −$11,000. (Expected value, Breaking ground)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=135"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Site leadership wants crews to stop waiting for materials, drawings and scaffolding. Which approach breaks…","x":"Site leadership wants crews to stop waiting for materials, drawings and scaffolding. Which approach breaks construction into constraint-free installation work packages? Answer: Advanced work packaging (AWP). AWP aligns engineering and procurement to the construction path. IWPs are only released when all constraints are cleared. (Advanced work packaging, Breaking ground)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=136"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"The site wants foremen to commit only to work that is truly ready, and to track percent plan complete (PPC)…","x":"The site wants foremen to commit only to work that is truly ready, and to track percent plan complete (PPC) weekly. Which lean method is this? Answer: Last Planner System. Last Planner uses pull planning, a make-ready lookahead, weekly commitments and PPC with reasons for variance. (Last Planner System, Breaking ground)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=137"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"In a weekly review, foremen report that 40% of their crews' time is spent waiting for permits and tools.…","x":"In a weekly review, foremen report that 40% of their crews' time is spent waiting for permits and tools. Which lean idea does this point to? Answer: Waste from waiting; remove the constraints causing it. Waiting is one of lean's classic wastes. Remove the systemic constraints rather than pushing the crews harder. (Lean construction, Breaking ground)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=138"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Mei and the QC lead disagree on the hydrostatic test approach for Unit 1 primary piping. The test is weeks…","x":"Mei and the QC lead disagree on the hydrostatic test approach for Unit 1 primary piping. The test is weeks away and both have valid points. Which conflict technique is most effective here? Answer: Collaborate to reach a solution both can support. With time available and two valid positions, collaborating gives a lasting win-win and buy-in. PMI rates collaborate/problem-solve as the best long-term technique. (Conflict management, Nuclear island construction)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=139"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"The engineering functional manager wants to pull your best welding engineer for a month to support an…","x":"The engineering functional manager wants to pull your best welding engineer for a month to support an outage at another station, right in the middle of Unit 1 piping. What should you do first? Answer: Meet the functional manager, understand their need and negotiate an option. In a matrix organization the PM negotiates for resources. Understand their need and look for a win-win, such as sharing time or a later start. (Negotiation, Nuclear island construction)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=140"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"The Unit 1 containment team has worked heavy overtime for six weeks and weld rejection rates are climbing.…","x":"The Unit 1 containment team has worked heavy overtime for six weeks and weld rejection rates are climbing. What should you do? Answer: Reassess the workload and negotiate scope, schedule or resources. Sustainable pace matters. Overtime this long hurts quality and morale. Re-plan with stakeholders using options. (Occupational burnout, Nuclear island construction)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=141"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Two Unit 1 area supervisors have a personal conflict that is now affecting their crews' work. They've tried…","x":"Two Unit 1 area supervisors have a personal conflict that is now affecting their crews' work. They've tried to resolve it themselves and failed. What should you do? Answer: Meet with both together and facilitate a resolution focused on the work. Once direct resolution fails, the PM steps in and facilitates. Keep it about the behaviour and the work, not the personalities. (Conflict management, Nuclear island construction)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=142"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Two contractors in the Unit 1 turbine hall are arguing over who is responsible for a gap between their…","x":"Two contractors in the Unit 1 turbine hall are arguing over who is responsible for a gap between their scopes, and work has stopped. What should you do first? Answer: Bring both together, review the contract scopes and agree the gap. Facilitate, using the contract documents as the shared facts. Then capture the decision through change control if the scope changes. (Conflict management, Nuclear island construction)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=143"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Mei, normally a top performer, seems disengaged lately. What should you do?","x":"Mei, normally a top performer, seems disengaged lately. What should you do? Answer: Have a one-on-one to learn what changed and what would help. Understand individual motivation through direct conversation. The cause could be growth, recognition, or something personal. (Motivation, Nuclear island construction)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=144"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Conflict between the rigging lead and the heavy-lift contractor is escalating, and a decision is needed…","x":"Conflict between the rigging lead and the heavy-lift contractor is escalating, and a decision is needed within the hour for the steam generator lift. Which technique is most appropriate? Answer: Force the decision, based on the safety requirements. Forcing has its place: emergencies, safety and urgent decisions. PMI accepts it when time is short and the stakes are high. (Conflict management, Nuclear island construction)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=145"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Two engineers agree to split the difference on a pour-sequence tolerance, leaving both somewhat unhappy.…","x":"Two engineers agree to split the difference on a pour-sequence tolerance, leaving both somewhat unhappy. This technique is: Answer: Compromise / reconcile. Compromise is lose-lose-ish: each side gives something up. It's useful for a temporary or partial solution. (Conflict management, Nuclear island construction)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=146"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"You hear that a key Unit 1 field engineer is planning to quit because of a conflict with an area lead. What…","x":"You hear that a key Unit 1 field engineer is planning to quit because of a conflict with an area lead. What should you do? Answer: Meet them privately to understand the issue and work on a resolution. Address it early and directly with the affected person. Facts first. (Conflict management, Nuclear island construction)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=147"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"A Unit 1 field engineer regularly helps other crews while their own work packages slip. What should you do?","x":"A Unit 1 field engineer regularly helps other crews while their own work packages slip. What should you do? Answer: Recognize their help and work with them to balance their own commitments. Value the behaviour, but help them balance it. Maybe the help itself needs to be planned into the work. (Team, Nuclear island construction)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=148"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"The Unit 1 intake screenhouse package shows EV = $400k, PV = $500k and AC = $450k. What is its status?","x":"The Unit 1 intake screenhouse package shows EV = $400k, PV = $500k and AC = $450k. What is its status? Answer: Behind schedule and over budget. SV = EV − PV = −$100k (behind). CV = EV − AC = −$50k (over budget). SPI = 0.8 and CPI ≈ 0.89, both below 1. (Earned value management, Nuclear island construction)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=149"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Northshore's site security systems package has BAC = $1,000,000 and CPI = 0.8. Assuming the current cost…","x":"Northshore's site security systems package has BAC = $1,000,000 and CPI = 0.8. Assuming the current cost performance continues, what is the EAC? Answer: $1,250,000. EAC = BAC / CPI = 1,000,000 / 0.8 = $1,250,000. (Earned value management, Nuclear island construction)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=150"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"The temporary construction power package has EAC = $1.2M and AC to date = $700k. What is the ETC?","x":"The temporary construction power package has EAC = $1.2M and AC to date = $700k. What is the ETC? Answer: $500k. ETC = EAC − AC = 1.2M − 0.7M = $500k. (Earned value management, Nuclear island construction)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=151"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"The site drainage package has BAC = $200k, EV = $120k and AC = $150k. What TCPI is needed to finish within…","x":"The site drainage package has BAC = $200k, EV = $120k and AC = $150k. What TCPI is needed to finish within BAC? Answer: 1.6. TCPI = (BAC − EV) / (BAC − AC) = 80k / 50k = 1.6. Very hard to achieve. (Earned value management, Nuclear island construction)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=152"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Operations asks for an extra valve-access platform in the Unit 1 reactor auxiliary building, which is under…","x":"Operations asks for an extra valve-access platform in the Unit 1 reactor auxiliary building, which is under a frozen baseline. What should you do first? Answer: Evaluate its impact on scope, schedule, cost, quality and risk. PMI's order: evaluate the impact, look at options, then submit to the CCB for a decision, then update plans and communicate. (Change control, Nuclear island construction)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=153"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"The CCB approves the new valve-access platform. What should you do next?","x":"The CCB approves the new valve-access platform. What should you do next? Answer: Update the baseline and plans, then tell stakeholders. Approved changes update the baselines and documents, get communicated, and then get implemented. (Change control, Nuclear island construction)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=154"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"The site concrete batch plant's compressive-strength control chart shows seven consecutive points on one…","x":"The site concrete batch plant's compressive-strength control chart shows seven consecutive points on one side of the mean, within the control limits. What does this indicate? Answer: The process may be out of control and should be investigated. The rule of seven signals a non-random pattern even inside the limits. Investigate for an assignable cause. (Control chart, Nuclear island construction)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=155"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Weld rework on Unit 1 piping is rising. Which tool helps prioritize the defect causes behind most of it?","x":"Weld rework on Unit 1 piping is rising. Which tool helps prioritize the defect causes behind most of it? Answer: Pareto chart. The Pareto chart ranks causes by frequency, so you can focus on the vital few (80/20). (Pareto principle, Nuclear island construction)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=156"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"The team wants to find possible root causes of the Unit 1 weld rework. Which tool is best?","x":"The team wants to find possible root causes of the Unit 1 weld rework. Which tool is best? Answer: Cause-and-effect (Ishikawa) diagram. Fishbone diagrams organize possible causes into categories (methods, materials, people, machines and so on). (Ishikawa diagram, Nuclear island construction)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=157"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"The quality manager is building Northshore's cost of quality report. Which is a cost of conformance?","x":"The quality manager is building Northshore's cost of quality report. Which is a cost of conformance? Answer: Training and inspection. Conformance costs (prevention and appraisal) are spent to avoid failures: training, testing, inspection. (Cost of quality, Nuclear island construction)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=158"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"The regulator asks how Northshore's quality program works. What is the main difference between quality…","x":"The regulator asks how Northshore's quality program works. What is the main difference between quality assurance and quality control? Answer: QA looks at the process; QC inspects the product. QA (manage quality) audits and improves processes. QC (control quality) inspects deliverables for correctness. (Quality assurance, Nuclear island construction)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=159"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"A field engineer installs a valve to drawing Rev C, but Rev D was issued last week. Which process failed?","x":"A field engineer installs a valve to drawing Rev C, but Rev D was issued last week. Which process failed? Answer: Configuration management and document control. Configuration management makes sure the right revision of every document reaches the work front. (Configuration management, Nuclear island construction)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=160"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"A planned review checks whether the Unit 1 piping contractor is following its own approved welding…","x":"A planned review checks whether the Unit 1 piping contractor is following its own approved welding procedures. This is a: Answer: Quality audit. Audits check process compliance (QA). Inspections check the product (QC). (Quality audit, Nuclear island construction)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=161"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"A QC inspector examines completed welds by radiography against acceptance criteria. Which process is this?","x":"A QC inspector examines completed welds by radiography against acceptance criteria. Which process is this? Answer: Control quality. Inspecting deliverables against standards is control quality. (Inspection, Nuclear island construction)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=162"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"The Northshore digital team building the field work-package app has been blocked for a week waiting on a…","x":"The Northshore digital team building the field work-package app has been blocked for a week waiting on a cybersecurity review. As the team's servant leader, what do you do? Answer: Work directly with the security group to remove the impediment. A servant leader's core job is removing impediments so the team can deliver. Go and clear the blocker. (Servant leadership, The digital field app)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=163"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"The field-app team, a self-organizing agile squad, asks you to decide how to split up the sprint tasks.…","x":"The field-app team, a self-organizing agile squad, asks you to decide how to split up the sprint tasks. What should you do? Answer: Encourage the team to decide together, and facilitate if needed. Self-organizing teams own how the work gets done. The PM or scrum master coaches them to decide and does not take the decision back. (Self-organization, The digital field app)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=164"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"In the field-app team's retrospectives nobody speaks up, but you hear complaints in the site-office hallway…","x":"In the field-app team's retrospectives nobody speaks up, but you hear complaints in the site-office hallway afterward. What is the best approach? Answer: Build safety: anonymous input, you speak last, and act visibly on what's raised. People speak up when it feels safe and when they see action. Anonymous tools and following through build that trust. (Psychological safety, The digital field app)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=165"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"The field-app team needs to make a quick decision about the offline mode, and you want to check everyone's…","x":"The field-app team needs to make a quick decision about the offline mode, and you want to check everyone's level of support. Which technique fits best? Answer: Fist of five. Fist of five (or roman voting) quickly shows the level of agreement and lets low scores explain their concerns. (Fist of five, The digital field app)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=166"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"During the field-app team's daily standup, one developer keeps taking over to solve technical problems, and…","x":"During the field-app team's daily standup, one developer keeps taking over to solve technical problems, and the meeting runs 40 minutes. What should the scrum master do? Answer: Coach the member privately on the standup's purpose; take problems offline. Standups are timeboxed coordination (15 minutes). Problem-solving happens afterward with only the people who need to be there. Coach privately. (Daily stand-up meeting, The digital field app)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=167"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Mid-sprint, a field-app developer wants to build an interesting 3D viewer feature that isn't in the sprint…","x":"Mid-sprint, a field-app developer wants to build an interesting 3D viewer feature that isn't in the sprint backlog. What should happen? Answer: Point to the sprint goal; suggest they take the idea to the product owner. Sprint scope is protected by the sprint goal. New ideas go to the product owner for the product backlog. (Scrum (software development), The digital field app)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=168"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"The field-app team keeps missing its sprint goals because it over-commits. As servant leader, what should…","x":"The field-app team keeps missing its sprint goals because it over-commits. As servant leader, what should you do? Answer: Coach the team to plan to its actual velocity and discuss it in the retro. Coach, don't control. Use empirical data (velocity) and the retrospective to improve planning. (Coaching, The digital field app)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=169"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"After a failed field-app release took down work-package printing for a shift, management wants the…","x":"After a failed field-app release took down work-package printing for a shift, management wants the retrospective to find who was responsible. What should the scrum master do? Answer: Keep it blameless and on the process, then share the improvement actions. Retrospectives work when they are safe and blameless. Focus on the system and the process, then share the outcomes. (Retrospective, The digital field app)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=170"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"The field-app team asks for a dedicated site-office room with whiteboards to collaborate. What should you do?","x":"The field-app team asks for a dedicated site-office room with whiteboards to collaborate. What should you do? Answer: Support it and work to arrange the space. Osmotic communication and information radiators help team performance. The servant leader secures that kind of support. (Agile software development, The digital field app)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=171"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"The future operations manager keeps asking field-app developers directly for small changes. What should you…","x":"The future operations manager keeps asking field-app developers directly for small changes. What should you do? Answer: Talk with the stakeholder about the change process and why it protects them. Explain the process without damaging the relationship. Changes go through the agreed route (backlog or change control). (Stakeholder engagement, The digital field app)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=172"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"A complex module of the field app is late and full of defects. A senior developer suggests pairing with a…","x":"A complex module of the field app is late and full of defects. A senior developer suggests pairing with a junior. What should you do? Answer: Support pairing, since it improves quality and spreads knowledge. Pairing improves quality and knowledge transfer. Fewer defects often makes up for the extra time spent. (Pair programming, The digital field app)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=173"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Field supervisors are not showing up to the requirements workshops for the field app. What should you do?","x":"Field supervisors are not showing up to the requirements workshops for the field app. What should you do? Answer: Understand why, then adjust: shorter sessions, their location, or sponsor backing. Find the root cause, then adapt the engagement approach to suit the stakeholders. (Requirements elicitation, The digital field app)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=174"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"The field-app team's scrum master asks you which behaviour best shows servant leadership. Which is it?","x":"The field-app team's scrum master asks you which behaviour best shows servant leadership. Which is it? Answer: Removing obstacles and supporting team growth. Servant leaders put the team's needs first: they remove impediments, coach and shield the team from distractions. (Servant leadership, The digital field app)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=175"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"The future operations manager threatens to withdraw support for the field app unless an extra feature is…","x":"The future operations manager threatens to withdraw support for the field app unless an extra feature is added. What should you do first? Answer: Understand the need, then evaluate it through the change process. Find the underlying need, then assess the impact properly. Neither giving in nor refusing outright is right. (Stakeholder management, The digital field app)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=176"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"The utility decides Northshore's digital tools will be delivered with agile, and the team, used to…","x":"The utility decides Northshore's digital tools will be delivered with agile, and the team, used to predictive work, is anxious. What should you do? Answer: Explain the why, train and coach, and start with a pilot. Change needs a reason, capability building and early wins. A pilot reduces risk. (Organizational culture, The digital field app)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=177"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"A field-app developer adds an extra feature they think supervisors will love, without telling anyone. This is:","x":"A field-app developer adds an extra feature they think supervisors will love, without telling anyone. This is: Answer: Gold plating. Gold plating means adding unrequested features. It adds risk, cost and possible defects without authorization. (Scope creep, The digital field app)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=178"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"On the field-app team's Kanban board, the 'testing' column always has many items waiting. What should the…","x":"On the field-app team's Kanban board, the 'testing' column always has many items waiting. What should the team do? Answer: Lower the WIP limit and swarm to clear the bottleneck. WIP limits expose bottlenecks. The team stops starting and starts finishing. (Kanban (development), The digital field app)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=179"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"The field-app sprint burndown is flat for the first three days. What does this most likely indicate?","x":"The field-app sprint burndown is flat for the first three days. What does this most likely indicate? Answer: Work isn't being finished, or the board isn't updated. A flat burndown means no remaining work is being burned. Check for blockers or update habits. (Burndown chart, The digital field app)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=180"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Field-app velocity over the last three sprints was 30, 34 and 32. The backlog has 160 points left. Roughly…","x":"Field-app velocity over the last three sprints was 30, 34 and 32. The backlog has 160 points left. Roughly how many sprints remain? Answer: 5. Average velocity ≈ 32. 160 / 32 = 5 sprints. (Velocity (software development), The digital field app)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=181"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Management wants to compare velocity across Northshore's three digital teams to rank them. What should you…","x":"Management wants to compare velocity across Northshore's three digital teams to rank them. What should you advise? Answer: Velocity is team-specific and shouldn't be compared. Story points are relative to each team. Comparing velocities is meaningless and invites point inflation. (Velocity (software development), The digital field app)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=182"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Which is the best-written user story for the Northshore field app?","x":"Which is the best-written user story for the Northshore field app? Answer: As a site supervisor, I want tomorrow's work packages so I can plan crews. The 'As a … I want … so that …' format captures the user, the need and the value. (User story, The digital field app)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=183"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Why would the field-app team use planning poker to estimate stories?","x":"Why would the field-app team use planning poker to estimate stories? Answer: It reduces anchoring and builds shared understanding. Simultaneous reveals avoid anchoring, and differing estimates trigger useful discussion. (Planning poker, The digital field app)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=184"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Why would the field-app team release a minimum viable product to a single crew first?","x":"Why would the field-app team release a minimum viable product to a single crew first? Answer: To test assumptions with real users, with little effort. An MVP tests the hypothesis and gets early feedback, which reduces the risk of building the wrong thing. (Minimum viable product, The digital field app)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=185"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"What is the main purpose of the field-app team's sprint review?","x":"What is the main purpose of the field-app team's sprint review? Answer: To inspect the increment with stakeholders and adapt the backlog. The sprint review is a working session with stakeholders on the product. The retrospective is about the process. (Sprint review, The digital field app)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=186"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"A field-app story meets its acceptance criteria but hasn't been code reviewed, which the definition of done…","x":"A field-app story meets its acceptance criteria but hasn't been code reviewed, which the definition of done requires. Is it done? Answer: No; it must meet the definition of done too. Done means it meets both the story's acceptance criteria and the team's shared definition of done. (Definition of done, The digital field app)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=187"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Your only commissioning expert for the turbine control system is retiring in three weeks. What should you do?","x":"Your only commissioning expert for the turbine control system is retiring in three weeks. What should you do? Answer: Plan knowledge transfer: pairing, recorded walkthroughs and repository updates. Tacit knowledge needs people-to-people methods (pairing, shadowing) plus explicit capture. Plan it now. (Knowledge transfer, Commissioning and startup)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=188"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"The stakeholder register lists Northshore's future station operations manager as 'unaware', but their team…","x":"The stakeholder register lists Northshore's future station operations manager as 'unaware', but their team will run the plant. What should you do? Answer: Plan engagement to move them toward supportive, starting with a meeting. The stakeholder engagement assessment matrix compares current and desired engagement. Close the gap on purpose, with a plan. (Stakeholder engagement, Commissioning and startup)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=189"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"By commissioning, your core team has moved into the performing stage. What is the appropriate leadership…","x":"By commissioning, your core team has moved into the performing stage. What is the appropriate leadership style? Answer: Delegating. Performing teams are mature. Delegate and support. (Team, Commissioning and startup)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=190"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Construction turns over the Unit 1 feedwater system to commissioning with 300 open punch items. What should…","x":"Construction turns over the Unit 1 feedwater system to commissioning with 300 open punch items. What should the PM ensure? Answer: Turnover follows agreed criteria: punch items sorted, owned and tracked. Clear turnover criteria (A/B/C punch categories), ownership and tracking keep commissioning moving without hiding problems. (Commissioning, Commissioning and startup)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=191"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"When should lessons learned be captured on Northshore?","x":"When should lessons learned be captured on Northshore? Answer: Throughout the project, applied to the units still to come. Lessons are gathered continuously. On a multi-unit build, Unit 1 lessons should directly improve Units 2–4. (Lessons learned, Commissioning and startup)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=192"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"During Unit 1 hot functional testing, a pump fails to meet its flow acceptance criteria. What should you do…","x":"During Unit 1 hot functional testing, a pump fails to meet its flow acceptance criteria. What should you do first? Answer: Document the deficiency, assess its impact and resolve it under the test process. Test results against acceptance criteria are quality control. Deficiencies are documented, dispositioned and tracked. (Commissioning, Commissioning and startup)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=193"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Fuel load for Unit 1 is in four weeks. The commissioning lead flags that 15% of the systems are behind on…","x":"Fuel load for Unit 1 is in four weeks. The commissioning lead flags that 15% of the systems are behind on turnover. What should you do? Answer: Analyze the schedule impact, plan recovery with the team, report to governance. Analyze, develop options, and communicate transparently. That is the PMI sequence. (Risk management, Commissioning and startup)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=194"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Operations formally accepts the completed Unit 1 systems. What is the output of the validate scope process?","x":"Operations formally accepts the completed Unit 1 systems. What is the output of the validate scope process? Answer: Accepted deliverables. Validate scope means the customer formally accepts deliverables that QC has already verified. (Validate scope, Handover and benefits)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=195"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Unit 4 is in service and all deliverables are accepted. What should you do before releasing the team?","x":"Unit 4 is in service and all deliverables are accepted. What should you do before releasing the team? Answer: Finish closure: final report, archived records, closed contracts, lessons. Closure includes contract closure, records, the final report, lessons learned and then releasing resources. (Project closure, Handover and benefits)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=196"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"A contractor has finished the work, but its final invoice disputes remain open. Can the project be formally…","x":"A contractor has finished the work, but its final invoice disputes remain open. Can the project be formally closed? Answer: Not until the claims are settled and the contract is formally closed. Close procurements requires resolving claims and formally completing the contract before administrative closure. (Contract, Handover and benefits)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=197"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"At handover, operations needs Northshore's design and construction records for 60+ years of operation. What…","x":"At handover, operations needs Northshore's design and construction records for 60+ years of operation. What is the PM's responsibility? Answer: Make sure records are complete, controlled and turned over to standard. Nuclear records retention is a regulatory requirement. The as-built record is part of the deliverable. (Records management, Handover and benefits)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=198"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Two years after Unit 1's in-service date, its capacity factor is below the business case. Who should lead…","x":"Two years after Unit 1's in-service date, its capacity factor is below the business case. Who should lead the response? Answer: The benefits owner in operations, using the business case metrics. After handover, operations owns benefit realization and corrective action. (Benefits realisation management, Handover and benefits)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=199"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Which is the best indicator that Northshore's transition to operations succeeded?","x":"Which is the best indicator that Northshore's transition to operations succeeded? Answer: Operations runs the units safely and reliably with what was handed over. Transition success is measured by the receiving organization's ability to sustain value. (Transition management, Handover and benefits)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=200"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Your best scheduler's functional manager wants her back full time for another project next month, in the…","x":"Your best scheduler's functional manager wants her back full time for another project next month, in the middle of your baseline update. You have no authority over her manager. What should you do? Answer: Meet her manager, show the impact on your milestone, and negotiate timing or a handover. In a weak matrix, influence and negotiation are your main tools: show the business impact and agree a solution, escalating only if that fails. (Weak matrix organization, Building the owner's team)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=201"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"The mayor will announce support for Northshore only if you commit to a local hiring target of 40%, but the…","x":"The mayor will announce support for Northshore only if you commit to a local hiring target of 40%, but the union agreement allows 25%. The announcement is tomorrow. What should you do? Answer: Explain the union limit, and offer what you can commit to, such as training for local hires. Don't commit to what you can't deliver. Be open about the constraint and offer real alternatives that serve the mayor's goal. (Stakeholder engagement, Licensing and the community)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=202"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"The safety manager stops all work at height after a near miss. The construction manager says this costs…","x":"The safety manager stops all work at height after a near miss. The construction manager says this costs $400k a day and wants work restarted this afternoon. As PM, what do you do? Answer: Support the stop, and work with both on what must be true to restart safely, fast. Safety stops are respected. The PM's job is to get to a safe restart quickly: clear conditions, owners and a time to review. (Conflict management, Building the owner's team)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=203"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Three of your project managers each want the program's single best estimator for their own proposal due the…","x":"Three of your project managers each want the program's single best estimator for their own proposal due the same week. As program manager, what should you do? Answer: Rank the proposals by value and deadline, then share the estimator to match. A program manager allocates shared resources by program priority. Make the trade-off openly, based on value and timing. (Program management, Building the owner's team)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=204"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"The crane for the reactor dome lift is booked for day 40, but the ring beam it needs finishes on day 44.…","x":"The crane for the reactor dome lift is booked for day 40, but the ring beam it needs finishes on day 44. The crane goes to another site on day 42 for three months. What should the construction manager do first? Answer: Look for a way to finish the ring beam by day 40, or move the lift, before the crane leaves. The crane is a hard constraint: check recovery options on the ring beam (crashing, overtime, resequencing) and the crane's availability before accepting a three-month delay. (Critical path method, Breaking ground)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=205"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Two work fronts both need the site's only 300-tonne crawler crane in weeks 12 to 14. One has 3 weeks of…","x":"Two work fronts both need the site's only 300-tonne crawler crane in weeks 12 to 14. One has 3 weeks of float; the other is critical. What should the project manager do? Answer: Give the crane to the critical work and move the other front into its float. Resource levelling uses float: the critical front keeps the crane, the front with 3 weeks of float moves without delaying the finish. (Resource leveling, Planning the build)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=206"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Unit 2's containment is 40% done, with 52% of its budget spent and the schedule 3 weeks behind. The program…","x":"Unit 2's containment is 40% done, with 52% of its budget spent and the schedule 3 weeks behind. The program director asks for one number to watch next month. Which is most useful? Answer: CPI, because it shows how much work each dollar is buying. CPI = EV / AC = 0.40 / 0.52 ≈ 0.77: every dollar buys 77 cents of work. Spend alone and headcount say nothing about value earned. (Earned value management, Nuclear island construction)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=207"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"As program manager over four units, you can fund one of two fixes: a design fix that removes a recurring…","x":"As program manager over four units, you can fund one of two fixes: a design fix that removes a recurring weld defect on Units 2 to 4, or overtime that recovers Unit 1's six-week slip. The money covers only one. What should guide the choice? Answer: The benefit to the whole program, comparing total cost, risk and value across the units. A program manager optimizes across projects: weigh the recurring cost of the weld defect on three units against the value of six weeks on Unit 1. (Program management, Commissioning and startup)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=208"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"A 600 m³ pour is ready at 6 a.m., but the inspector who must release the hold point is stuck in traffic for…","x":"A 600 m³ pour is ready at 6 a.m., but the inspector who must release the hold point is stuck in traffic for two hours. The concrete trucks are already batching. What should the superintendent do? Answer: Hold the pour, call the plant to slow batching, and wait for the release. A hold point can only be released by the named inspector. Manage the trucks (slow or divert batching); never pour through a hold point. (Hold point (quality), Breaking ground)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=209"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"The turbine vendor warns that a sub-supplier's factory fire will delay the stator by 10 weeks. The stator…","x":"The turbine vendor warns that a sub-supplier's factory fire will delay the stator by 10 weeks. The stator has 4 weeks of float. What should the project manager do first? Answer: Confirm the new date, assess the 6-week impact and work through recovery options with the vendor. Get facts first: a firm date, the impact beyond float (10 - 4 = 6 weeks) and recovery options (expediting, resequencing, partial shipments). (Supply chain risk management, Contracts and supply chain)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=210"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Fish spawning season closes in-water work from April 1. The cofferdam is 85% done on March 20, and…","x":"Fish spawning season closes in-water work from April 1. The cofferdam is 85% done on March 20, and finishing needs 18 more working days. What should the project manager do? Answer: Plan to make the cofferdam safe by March 31 and finish after the window reopens. The fisheries window is a legal constraint. Secure the partial work for the closure and replan the rest; don't plan to break the permit. (Environmental compliance, Breaking ground)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=211"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Field engineers say the embed plates in the drawings clash with rebar at 40 locations. Fixing the drawings…","x":"Field engineers say the embed plates in the drawings clash with rebar at 40 locations. Fixing the drawings takes 3 weeks; the walls are scheduled in 2. What should you do? Answer: Have engineering resolve the first walls' clashes first, and resequence the walls to match. Prioritize the design fix to suit the construction sequence: the first walls get resolved first, and the order adapts, so work continues without unapproved field changes. (Constructability, Nuclear island construction)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=212"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"A pump failed its flow test twice. The vendor says it will pass after a third test with a tweaked setting.…","x":"A pump failed its flow test twice. The vendor says it will pass after a third test with a tweaked setting. Startup is in 9 days. What should the commissioning manager do? Answer: Treat it as a deficiency: find the cause with the vendor before any retest. Two failures mean a cause to understand. Retesting until it passes, or accepting below the criteria, hides the problem. (Commissioning, Commissioning and startup)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=213"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"The sponsor asks you to cut 8 weeks. Fast-tracking the turbine hall saves 6 weeks with some rework risk;…","x":"The sponsor asks you to cut 8 weeks. Fast-tracking the turbine hall saves 6 weeks with some rework risk; crashing the electrical works saves 4 weeks for $3M. What should you present? Answer: Options with their savings, cost and risk, and a recommended combination. Give the sponsor a real decision: each option's time, cost and risk, and a combination that meets 8 weeks (for example fast-track plus partial crashing). (Schedule compression, Planning the build)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=214"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"A contractor starts extra excavation without a signed change order, saying your site engineer told them…","x":"A contractor starts extra excavation without a signed change order, saying your site engineer told them verbally to go ahead. What should the project manager do? Answer: Check what was instructed, stop unapproved work, and process a proper change order. Establish the facts, control further unauthorized work, and handle the valid part through the contract's change process. (Change order, Contracts and supply chain)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=215"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Unit 1 finished 4 months late, mostly from late vendor drawings. Unit 2 starts design next month with the…","x":"Unit 1 finished 4 months late, mostly from late vendor drawings. Unit 2 starts design next month with the same vendor. What should the program manager do first? Answer: Build the drawing-delivery lesson into Unit 2's contract and schedule now. Lessons matter when they change the next plan: put drawing milestones, incentives and float where they address the cause. (Lessons learned, Handover and benefits)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=216"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"The field app's sprint can deliver either offline mode (foremen in the turbine hall have no signal) or a…","x":"The field app's sprint can deliver either offline mode (foremen in the turbine hall have no signal) or a dashboard the program director asked for. Which should the product owner choose first? Answer: Offline mode, because without it the foremen can't use the app at all. Value comes from the app being used: offline mode unblocks the core users. The dashboard can follow. (Minimum viable product, The digital field app)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=217"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"A heavy-lift path crosses an energized 230 kV line. The utility can de-energize it for 6 hours on two…","x":"A heavy-lift path crosses an energized 230 kV line. The utility can de-energize it for 6 hours on two Sundays. The lift plan needs 8 hours. What should you do? Answer: Rework the lift plan to fit inside the outage windows, or ask for longer outages. The outage window is the constraint: replan the lift (pre-rigging, sequence) to fit, or negotiate a longer outage. Never lift near an energized line. (Risk management, Nuclear island construction)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=218"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"A 200-person crew is on site, but only 60 have work they can start: the rest are waiting for scaffolding,…","x":"A 200-person crew is on site, but only 60 have work they can start: the rest are waiting for scaffolding, permits or materials. What should the construction manager do first? Answer: Find and clear the top constraints, and match crew size to the work ready to start. Work that isn't constraint-free wastes labour. Remove the biggest constraints first and size crews to ready work (the logic of advanced work packaging and Last Planner). (Constraint management, Breaking ground)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=219"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"A month before startup, a contractor's crew starts skipping lockout steps to save time, and the…","x":"A month before startup, a contractor's crew starts skipping lockout steps to save time, and the commissioning schedule is tight. What should the project manager do? Answer: Stop the unsafe practice now, find why it started, and fix the schedule pressure. Lockout is life-critical. Stop it, and look at the cause: schedule pressure that pushes people to skip steps must be managed, not ignored. (Safety culture, Commissioning and startup)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=220"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Units 3 and 4 won't start for four years, but the board wants a single fixed completion date for all four…","x":"Units 3 and 4 won't start for four years, but the board wants a single fixed completion date for all four units today. What should the program manager provide? Answer: A firm date for Units 1 and 2 and a range for 3 and 4, with when it will firm up. Give the board what you can stand behind: firm near-term dates, realistic ranges further out, and a plan to narrow them. (Rolling wave planning, Planning the build)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=221"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"The board can fund either a 2-year refurbishment that returns 9% with low risk, or a new unit that returns…","x":"The board can fund either a 2-year refurbishment that returns 9% with low risk, or a new unit that returns 14% with high schedule risk. As portfolio manager, what should drive the advice? Answer: Strategic fit and the portfolio's balance of risk and return, not one project's return alone. Portfolio decisions weigh strategy, capacity, risk appetite and balance across the whole portfolio, not a single headline number. (Portfolio management, The business case)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=222"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Your steering committee meets monthly, but a $12M change needs a decision within 10 days or the vendor's…","x":"Your steering committee meets monthly, but a $12M change needs a decision within 10 days or the vendor's price expires. What should you do? Answer: Use the expedited decision route, or ask the chair for a special meeting. Governance has to work at the speed of the project: use the agreed fast path rather than bypassing or missing the decision. (Governance, The business case)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=223"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Night-time pile driving would save 5 weeks, but the town's noise bylaw stops work after 9 p.m., and…","x":"Night-time pile driving would save 5 weeks, but the town's noise bylaw stops work after 9 p.m., and residents are already complaining. What should you do? Answer: Work within the bylaw, and look for quieter methods or day shifts to recover time. Bylaws and community trust are constraints. Recover time inside them: quieter methods such as vibro-piling, or longer day shifts. (Community engagement, Licensing and the community)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=224"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Where would you record who a stakeholder is, their interest and their influence?","x":"Where would you record who a stakeholder is, their interest and their influence? Answer: The stakeholder register. The stakeholder register lists stakeholders with their interests, influence and engagement levels. (Stakeholder register, CAPM: the foundations)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=225"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Which practice comes from Extreme Programming (XP)?","x":"Which practice comes from Extreme Programming (XP)? Answer: Pair programming. XP brought pair programming, test-first development and continuous integration. (Extreme programming, PMI-ACP: agile in practice)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=226"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"In AWP, what does a foreman get to build from?","x":"In AWP, what does a foreman get to build from? Answer: An installation work package (IWP). The IWP is the foreman's package: a week or two of work for one crew, with drawings, materials, tools and permits all in hand. (Advanced work packaging, Advanced work packaging)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=227"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"An IWP is ready to release, but the scaffold it needs won't be up until Thursday. What should happen?","x":"An IWP is ready to release, but the scaffold it needs won't be up until Thursday. What should happen? Answer: Hold it, and release a ready package instead. AWP's rule: only constraint-free packages go to the field. Releasing one with an open constraint creates the waiting and rework AWP exists to prevent. (Advanced work packaging, Advanced work packaging)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=228"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Why does AWP start planning from the construction sequence?","x":"Why does AWP start planning from the construction sequence? Answer: So engineering and supply follow the build order. Path of construction drives everything upstream: engineering work packages and deliveries are sequenced so the field gets what it needs, when it needs it. (Advanced work packaging, Advanced work packaging)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=229"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"A site tracks the share of crew hours spent on installation work packages that were constraint-free at…","x":"A site tracks the share of crew hours spent on installation work packages that were constraint-free at release. It sits at 55%. What does raising it mostly improve? Answer: Craft productivity. Industry studies credit AWP with productivity gains mainly from removing waiting: crews start on packages that are truly ready. (Advanced work packaging, Advanced work packaging)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=230"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Who usually builds and releases installation work packages on an AWP project?","x":"Who usually builds and releases installation work packages on an AWP project? Answer: Workface planners. Workface planners turn CWPs into IWPs, check constraints and release ready packages, in step with the field supervisors. (Advanced work packaging, Advanced work packaging)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=231"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"How big should an installation work package usually be?","x":"How big should an installation work package usually be? Answer: A week or two of work for one crew. Small enough to finish in a week or two, so constraints can be checked and progress is easy to measure. (Advanced work packaging, Advanced work packaging)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=232"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Which document formally authorizes a project and names the project manager?","x":"Which document formally authorizes a project and names the project manager? Answer: The project charter. The charter authorizes the project and gives the project manager authority to use resources. (Project charter, CAPM: the foundations)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=233"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"What is float on a schedule activity?","x":"What is float on a schedule activity? Answer: Delay it can take without moving the finish. Total float is how much an activity can be delayed without moving the project finish date. (Critical path method, CAPM: the foundations)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=234"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"A team member suggests a small extra feature. In a predictive project, where does the request go first?","x":"A team member suggests a small extra feature. In a predictive project, where does the request go first? Answer: Into change control. Every scope change, however small, goes through integrated change control. (Change control, CAPM: the foundations)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=235"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"On a Kanban board, what does a work-in-progress (WIP) limit do?","x":"On a Kanban board, what does a work-in-progress (WIP) limit do? Answer: Stops starting more than you can finish. WIP limits cap the work in each state, exposing bottlenecks and improving flow. (Kanban (development), PMI-ACP: agile in practice)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=236"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"What is the main purpose of a retrospective?","x":"What is the main purpose of a retrospective? Answer: To improve how the team works. Retrospectives are for the team to inspect and adapt its own process. (Retrospective, PMI-ACP: agile in practice)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=237"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"On a cumulative flow diagram, the band for 'testing' keeps getting wider. What does it suggest?","x":"On a cumulative flow diagram, the band for 'testing' keeps getting wider. What does it suggest? Answer: A bottleneck in testing. A widening band means more items are entering a state than leaving it: a bottleneck. (Cumulative flow diagram, PMI-ACP: agile in practice)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=238"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Two projects in your program both depend on one shared substation. Who should manage that dependency?","x":"Two projects in your program both depend on one shared substation. Who should manage that dependency? Answer: The program manager. Interdependencies between projects are the program manager's job: one view, one owner. (Program management, PgMP: running a program)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=239"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Two proposals score the same on value. One uses scarce welders the portfolio is already short of. What…","x":"Two proposals score the same on value. One uses scarce welders the portfolio is already short of. What should the portfolio manager weigh? Answer: Capacity: the welders actually available. Portfolios are limited by capacity. Selecting work that needs resources you don't have puts the whole portfolio at risk. (Portfolio management, PfMP: choosing the work)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=240"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Strategy changes, and one in-flight project no longer fits. What should the portfolio manager consider?","x":"Strategy changes, and one in-flight project no longer fits. What should the portfolio manager consider? Answer: Stopping or reshaping it. Sunk cost doesn't count: portfolios rebalance when strategy changes, including stopping work that no longer fits. (Portfolio management, PfMP: choosing the work)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=241"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Which measure tells a portfolio board the most about balance?","x":"Which measure tells a portfolio board the most about balance? Answer: The mix of risk, return and fit. Balance is about the mix: risk, return, fit and timing across the portfolio. (Portfolio management, PfMP: choosing the work)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=242"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"A threat has a 20% chance of costing $500k. An opportunity has a 30% chance of saving $200k. What is the…","x":"A threat has a 20% chance of costing $500k. An opportunity has a 30% chance of saving $200k. What is the combined EMV? Answer: −$40k. EMV = (0.2 × −500k) + (0.3 × +200k) = −100k + 60k = −40k. (Expected monetary value, PMI-RMP: risk, deeper)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=243"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"What is a risk trigger?","x":"What is a risk trigger? Answer: A warning sign that a risk is about to happen. Triggers are warning signs that a risk is occurring or about to, so the owner can act. (Risk management, PMI-RMP: risk, deeper)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=244"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Why run a Monte Carlo simulation on the schedule?","x":"Why run a Monte Carlo simulation on the schedule? Answer: To see the range of finish dates and how likely each is. Monte Carlo gives a distribution of outcomes (for example P50 and P80 dates), not one number. (Monte Carlo method, PMI-RMP: risk, deeper)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=245"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"An activity has total float of −5 days. What does that mean?","x":"An activity has total float of −5 days. What does that mean? Answer: It misses a deadline by 5 days. Negative float means the plan can't meet a constraint date: recovery is needed. (Critical path method, PMI-SP: scheduling, deeper)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=246"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"What is an open end in a schedule network?","x":"What is an open end in a schedule network? Answer: An activity missing a predecessor or a successor. Open ends break the logic: delays can't flow through, so the critical path is unreliable. (Schedule network analysis, PMI-SP: scheduling, deeper)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=247"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"SPI is 0.85. What does it tell you?","x":"SPI is 0.85. What does it tell you? Answer: Work is going at 85% of the planned rate. SPI = EV / PV: the rate of progress against the plan. It doesn't by itself predict the exact finish. (Schedule performance index, PMI-SP: scheduling, deeper)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=248"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"When should the schedule baseline change?","x":"When should the schedule baseline change? Answer: Only through approved change control. The baseline is the approved plan; it moves only through approved changes, so variances stay visible. (Schedule baseline, PMI-SP: scheduling, deeper)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=249"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"What makes a group of projects a program, rather than a portfolio?","x":"What makes a group of projects a program, rather than a portfolio? Answer: They're managed together for joint benefits. A program coordinates related projects for combined benefits; a portfolio is a collection chosen for strategy, not necessarily related. (Program management, PgMP: running a program)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=250"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Who is usually accountable for a program's benefits after the projects close?","x":"Who is usually accountable for a program's benefits after the projects close? Answer: The business owners who receive them. Benefits owners in the business are accountable for realizing them; the program sets up the transition. (Benefits realization, PgMP: running a program)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=251"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"A program's governance board should mainly do what?","x":"A program's governance board should mainly do what? Answer: Steer toward benefits and decide major changes. Program governance sets direction, approves major changes and checks benefits, leaving detail to the projects. (Governance, PgMP: running a program)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=252"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"What does a portfolio manager mainly do?","x":"What does a portfolio manager mainly do? Answer: Choose and balance work to fit strategy. Portfolio management selects, prioritizes and balances programs and projects against strategy and capacity. (Portfolio management, PfMP: choosing the work)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=253"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Why does an organization's risk appetite matter to the project?","x":"Why does an organization's risk appetite matter to the project? Answer: It sets how much risk is acceptable. Appetite and thresholds set the level of risk that is acceptable, shaping which risks need responses. (Risk appetite, PMI-RMP: risk, deeper)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=254"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Your best foreman says he's bored and is thinking of leaving for a pipeline job. What do you do?","x":"Your best foreman says he's bored and is thinking of leaving for a pipeline job. What do you do? Answer: Ask what he wants next, and give him a stretch role on the job. Motivation is about growth and challenge as much as money. Find out what he wants and give it a real path here. (Motivation, Building the owner's team)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=255"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"A town councillor posts on social media that the site's trucks are wrecking a road. Half of it is true.…","x":"A town councillor posts on social media that the site's trucks are wrecking a road. Half of it is true. What do you do? Answer: Own the true part, fix it, and tell people what you're doing. Credibility comes from owning what's real, acting on it, and keeping people informed. Then correct the rest calmly. (Stakeholder engagement, Licensing and the community)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=256"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Two supers keep fighting over the same crane. Each blames the other at every meeting. What's the root fix?","x":"Two supers keep fighting over the same crane. Each blames the other at every meeting. What's the root fix? Answer: Set a clear crane schedule and a way to settle clashes. The conflict is over a scarce resource. Fix the system (a shared schedule and rules) and the fighting fades. (Conflict management, Building the owner's team)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=257"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Forecast shows a week of freezing nights right when the slab pours are planned. What should you do first?","x":"Forecast shows a week of freezing nights right when the slab pours are planned. What should you do first? Answer: Plan cold-weather measures, or move the pours, before the week arrives. A known threat with a date gets a response now: heating and blankets, an adjusted mix, or a resequence. (Risk management, Breaking ground)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=258"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"CPI has been 0.80 for six months and nothing has changed. What's the most realistic forecast?","x":"CPI has been 0.80 for six months and nothing has changed. What's the most realistic forecast? Answer: Assume the trend holds: EAC = BAC / CPI. When performance is steady and nothing is changing, the cost trend is the better predictor: BAC / CPI. (Estimate at completion, Nuclear island construction)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=259"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"You need two weeks back. Adding a second shift costs $400k; overlapping design and procurement saves the…","x":"You need two weeks back. Adding a second shift costs $400k; overlapping design and procurement saves the same time but adds rework risk. What decides it? Answer: The cost of the shift against the expected cost of the rework. Compare like for like: the known cost of crashing against the probability-weighted cost of the rework from fast-tracking. (Schedule compression, Planning the build)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=260"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"The electrical sub prices a change at three times what your estimator expects. What should you do?","x":"The electrical sub prices a change at three times what your estimator expects. What should you do? Answer: Ask for their breakdown and negotiate it against your independent estimate. Use your own estimate to test theirs: ask for quantities, rates and hours, and negotiate on facts. (Change order, Contracts and supply chain)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=261"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"The program director wants a feature added to the field app's current sprint. What should the team do?","x":"The program director wants a feature added to the field app's current sprint. What should the team do? Answer: Send it to the product owner for the backlog. The sprint is protected; new requests go to the product owner, who orders the backlog. (Product backlog, The digital field app)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=262"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"A crew wants to skip a lockout step on a pump because 'it's only for ten minutes'. What do you say?","x":"A crew wants to skip a lockout step on a pump because 'it's only for ten minutes'. What do you say? Answer: No. Lock it out, every time. Lockout is life-critical. Time saved never justifies skipping it. (Lockout-tagout, Commissioning and startup)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=263"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"The project is done but two sub-contracts are still open with small claims. What's the right move?","x":"The project is done but two sub-contracts are still open with small claims. What's the right move? Answer: Settle and close the contracts before closing the project. Formal closure includes closing contracts. Settle the claims through the contract process, then close. (Project closure, Handover and benefits)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=264"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"A team grows from 5 to 10 people. How many communication channels are there now?","x":"A team grows from 5 to 10 people. How many communication channels are there now? Answer: 45. Channels = n(n-1)/2 = 10 × 9 / 2 = 45. Going from 5 (10 channels) to 10 more than quadruples them. (Communication channels, CAPM: the foundations)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=265"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"The daily standup has turned into a status report to the manager. What should change?","x":"The daily standup has turned into a status report to the manager. What should change? Answer: Make it the team's plan for the day, with the manager listening. The standup is for the team to coordinate, not to report upward. (Daily standup, PMI-ACP: agile in practice)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=266"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Your program's projects keep finishing on time, but the business still isn't seeing the promised savings.…","x":"Your program's projects keep finishing on time, but the business still isn't seeing the promised savings. What's wrong? Answer: Outputs are delivered, but nobody is driving the benefits. Delivering outputs isn't the same as realizing benefits. Someone has to own and drive adoption. (Program management, PgMP: running a program)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=267"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"A vendor may go bankrupt. You can't avoid them, but you can pay for a performance bond. Which response is…","x":"A vendor may go bankrupt. You can't avoid them, but you can pay for a performance bond. Which response is that? Answer: Transfer. A bond shifts the financial impact to a third party: transfer. (Risk response, PMI-RMP: risk, deeper)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=268"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"The schedule shows the job finishing on time, but nobody on site believes it. What should you check first?","x":"The schedule shows the job finishing on time, but nobody on site believes it. What should you check first? Answer: Whether the logic and durations reflect how the work is really being done. A schedule nobody believes usually has broken logic or unrealistic durations. Fix the model, then the trust follows. (Schedule baseline, PMI-SP: scheduling, deeper)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=269"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"A foreman finishes his IWP in three days instead of ten because everything was ready. What does that tell you?","x":"A foreman finishes his IWP in three days instead of ten because everything was ready. What does that tell you? Answer: Constraint-free packages work: release more of them. When constraints are cleared, crews fly. The answer is more ready packages, not suspicion. (Advanced work packaging, Advanced work packaging)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=270"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"A spill kit is missing from a fuel station on site, and an auditor is coming tomorrow. What do you do?","x":"A spill kit is missing from a fuel station on site, and an auditor is coming tomorrow. What do you do? Answer: Replace it now, and find out why it went missing. Fix the gap for real, then fix the cause so it doesn't happen again. (Environmental compliance, Breaking ground)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=271"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Someone proposes organizing the WBS by department instead of by deliverable. Why push back?","x":"Someone proposes organizing the WBS by department instead of by deliverable. Why push back? Answer: The WBS should show what gets delivered, not who does it. The WBS breaks down the deliverables; an organizational breakdown structure (OBS) shows who. (Work breakdown structure, Planning the build)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=272"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Halfway through, the reason for the project changes: the province no longer needs the extra power. What…","x":"Halfway through, the reason for the project changes: the province no longer needs the extra power. What should happen? Answer: Take it to governance to re-check the business case. When the reason for a project changes, governance decides whether it still makes sense: continue, change or stop. (Business case, The business case)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=273"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Every project in the portfolio is 'priority one'. What should the portfolio manager do?","x":"Every project in the portfolio is 'priority one'. What should the portfolio manager do? Answer: Force a real ranking against strategy and capacity. If everything is priority one, nothing is. Ranking forces the trade-offs strategy requires. (Portfolio management, PfMP: choosing the work)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=274"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"In PMI's CPMAI method, what is the first phase of an AI project?","x":"In PMI's CPMAI method, what is the first phase of an AI project? Answer: Business understanding. CPMAI starts with business understanding: what problem, what value, and whether AI is the right tool at all. (Artificial intelligence, PMI AI: managing AI projects)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=275"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Why does CPMAI put data understanding so early?","x":"Why does CPMAI put data understanding so early? Answer: AI projects are data projects: the data decides what's possible. Most AI projects fail on data, not algorithms. Knowing what data you have, and its quality, shapes everything after. (Data quality, PMI AI: managing AI projects)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=276"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Six months after go-live, the AI's forecasts get steadily worse. What's the likely cause?","x":"Six months after go-live, the AI's forecasts get steadily worse. What's the likely cause? Answer: The data has drifted away from what the model learned. Models learn from past data; when the world changes, accuracy drifts. Operations must monitor and retrain. (Concept drift, PMI AI: managing AI projects)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=277"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"CPMAI's motto for AI projects is 'think big, start small, iterate often'. What does 'start small' mean in…","x":"CPMAI's motto for AI projects is 'think big, start small, iterate often'. What does 'start small' mean in practice? Answer: Deliver a narrow, useful slice first, then grow. Small, real wins prove value and surface data problems early, before scaling up. (Artificial intelligence, PMI AI: managing AI projects)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=278"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"An AI tool flags a weld as defective, but nobody can say why. What should the team require before relying…","x":"An AI tool flags a weld as defective, but nobody can say why. What should the team require before relying on it? Answer: Enough explanation and human review to trust each call. In safety work, people stay accountable: decisions need explanation and human review, not blind trust. (Explainable artificial intelligence, PMI AI: managing AI projects)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=279"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Two site surveys don't line up: they're off by tens of metres. What's the most likely cause?","x":"Two site surveys don't line up: they're off by tens of metres. What's the most likely cause? Answer: They use different datums, such as NAD27 and NAD83. Mixing datums or coordinate systems is the classic cause of offsets of tens of metres; reproject to one system. (North American Datum, GIS on the job)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=280"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"You need every well within 500 m of a planned pipeline. Which GIS operation does it?","x":"You need every well within 500 m of a planned pipeline. Which GIS operation does it? Answer: A buffer around the route, then select the wells inside. A buffer draws the 500 m zone; a spatial select finds what falls inside it. (Buffer analysis, GIS on the job)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=281"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"How is a digital elevation model usually stored in GIS?","x":"How is a digital elevation model usually stored in GIS? Answer: As a raster grid of heights. Elevation is continuous, so it's stored as a raster: a grid where each cell holds a height. (Digital elevation model, GIS on the job)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=282"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Why link work packages to locations on a GIS map?","x":"Why link work packages to locations on a GIS map? Answer: Progress and problems show up where they are on site. Location-based views reveal clusters of delay or constraint that a list hides. (Geographic information system, GIS on the job)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=283"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"What is clash detection in BIM?","x":"What is clash detection in BIM? Answer: Finding where disciplines' models collide before building. Clash detection runs the federated model to find, say, a duct through a beam, before it costs anything on site. (Building information modeling, BIM: the model is the job)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=284"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"What does 4D BIM add to the 3D model?","x":"What does 4D BIM add to the 3D model? Answer: Time: the schedule linked to the model. 4D links model elements to the schedule, so you can watch the build sequence; 5D adds cost. (4D BIM, BIM: the model is the job)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=285"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"What is IFC in BIM?","x":"What is IFC in BIM? Answer: An open, vendor-neutral format for sharing models. IFC (ISO 16739) lets different software exchange model data without being locked to one vendor. (Industry Foundation Classes, BIM: the model is the job)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=286"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Under ISO 19650, where does project information live?","x":"Under ISO 19650, where does project information live? Answer: In a common data environment. ISO 19650 sets up a common data environment: one managed place for information with clear status and approvals. (ISO 19650, BIM: the model is the job)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=287"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"A model element is marked LOD 400. What does that tell you?","x":"A model element is marked LOD 400. What does that tell you? Answer: It's detailed enough for fabrication and installation. LOD (Level of Development) runs from 100 (concept) to 500 (as-built); 400 means fabrication-ready detail. (Building information modeling, BIM: the model is the job)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=288"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Which standard sets management system requirements for nuclear facilities in Canada?","x":"Which standard sets management system requirements for nuclear facilities in Canada? Answer: CSA N286. CSA N286 is the management system standard the CNSC expects licensees and their suppliers to follow. (CSA Group, Nuclear QA)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=289"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"How many criteria does 10 CFR 50 Appendix B set out for nuclear quality assurance?","x":"How many criteria does 10 CFR 50 Appendix B set out for nuclear quality assurance? Answer: 18. Appendix B has 18 criteria, from organization through audits; ASME NQA-1 is the common way to meet them. (Nuclear Regulatory Commission, Nuclear QA)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=290"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"A cheap commercial valve is needed for a safety-related system. What process lets it be used?","x":"A cheap commercial valve is needed for a safety-related system. What process lets it be used? Answer: Commercial grade dedication. Dedication verifies the item's critical characteristics so a commercial item can be accepted for safety-related use. (Quality assurance, Nuclear QA)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=291"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Bolts arrive with markings that look wrong. You suspect counterfeits. What first?","x":"Bolts arrive with markings that look wrong. You suspect counterfeits. What first? Answer: Quarantine them and report them as suspect items. Counterfeit, fraudulent and suspect items are quarantined, reported and investigated; they never go into the plant on a guess. (Counterfeit, Nuclear QA)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=292"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Which of these is a standard disposition for a nonconforming item?","x":"Which of these is a standard disposition for a nonconforming item? Answer: Use-as-is, repair, rework or reject. NQA-1 style programs disposition nonconformances as use-as-is, repair, rework or reject, with engineering justification where needed. (Nonconformance, Nuclear QA)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=293"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Under Ontario's construction regulation, fall protection is generally required when a worker could fall…","x":"Under Ontario's construction regulation, fall protection is generally required when a worker could fall more than: Answer: 3 metres. O. Reg. 213/91 generally requires fall protection where a worker may fall more than 3 m, and in other set cases, such as over operating machinery, at lower heights. (Fall protection, The Green Book (Ontario OHSA))","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=294"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"What are workers' three basic rights under Ontario's OHSA?","x":"What are workers' three basic rights under Ontario's OHSA? Answer: To know, to participate, and to refuse unsafe work. The internal responsibility system rests on the right to know, the right to participate and the right to refuse unsafe work. (Occupational Health and Safety Act (Ontario), The Green Book (Ontario OHSA))","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=295"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Under O. Reg. 213/91, trench walls generally need support (or sloping) once a trench is deeper than:","x":"Under O. Reg. 213/91, trench walls generally need support (or sloping) once a trench is deeper than: Answer: 1.2 metres. Walls of a trench more than 1.2 m deep must be supported, sloped or protected by a trench box, with some exceptions. (Trench, The Green Book (Ontario OHSA))","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=296"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"When must a constructor file a Notice of Project with the Ontario Ministry of Labour?","x":"When must a constructor file a Notice of Project with the Ontario Ministry of Labour? Answer: When the project's total cost is more than $50,000. O. Reg. 213/91 requires a notice for projects over $50,000, among other triggers such as certain demolitions. (Occupational Health and Safety Act (Ontario), The Green Book (Ontario OHSA))","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=297"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"A project regularly employs 25 workers. What does Ontario's OHSA require?","x":"A project regularly employs 25 workers. What does Ontario's OHSA require? Answer: A joint health and safety committee. A JHSC is required where 20 or more workers are regularly employed (for projects of three months or more); smaller projects of 6 or more need a health and safety representative. (Joint health and safety committee, The Green Book (Ontario OHSA))","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=298"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"In the V-model, what does the right side of the V represent?","x":"In the V-model, what does the right side of the V represent? Answer: Integration, verification and validation. The left side decomposes requirements into design; the right side integrates and tests back up against each level. (V-model, Systems engineering)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=299"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"What's the difference between verification and validation?","x":"What's the difference between verification and validation? Answer: Verification: built it right. Validation: built the right thing. Verification checks against the specification; validation checks the result meets the real need. (Verification and validation, Systems engineering)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=300"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Why keep a requirements traceability matrix?","x":"Why keep a requirements traceability matrix? Answer: So every requirement links to design, tests and proof it's met. Traceability shows nothing was missed and makes the impact of a change visible. (Traceability matrix, Systems engineering)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=301"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Two contractors' systems must connect at a pump skid. What document defines that connection?","x":"Two contractors' systems must connect at a pump skid. What document defines that connection? Answer: An interface control document. An ICD defines the physical, functional and data interfaces between systems, so both sides build to the same boundary. (Interface control document, Systems engineering)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=302"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"What makes a CANDU reactor different from most reactors?","x":"What makes a CANDU reactor different from most reactors? Answer: Heavy water moderator and natural uranium fuel. CANDU uses heavy water as moderator and coolant, which lets it burn natural (unenriched) uranium; it's also refuelled online. (CANDU reactor, Nuclear trivia)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=303"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Where and when did the first human-made nuclear chain reaction happen?","x":"Where and when did the first human-made nuclear chain reaction happen? Answer: Chicago, December 1942. Chicago Pile-1, under the stands of a squash court at the University of Chicago, went critical on 2 December 1942. (Chicago Pile-1, Nuclear trivia)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=304"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Which small modular reactor is being built at Darlington in Ontario?","x":"Which small modular reactor is being built at Darlington in Ontario? Answer: The GE Hitachi BWRX-300. OPG is building a BWRX-300 at Darlington, the first grid-scale SMR in the G7. (BWRX-300, Nuclear trivia)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=305"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"What does a moderator do in a reactor?","x":"What does a moderator do in a reactor? Answer: Slows neutrons so they cause more fission. Slow (thermal) neutrons are far more likely to split uranium-235, so the moderator keeps the chain reaction going. (Neutron moderator, Nuclear trivia)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=306"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"What type of reactor was at Chernobyl?","x":"What type of reactor was at Chernobyl? Answer: RBMK. Chernobyl Unit 4 was an RBMK, a graphite-moderated, water-cooled Soviet design. (RBMK, Nuclear trivia)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=307"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Hoover Dam's concrete was cooled with pipes of cold water. Without them, roughly how long would it have…","x":"Hoover Dam's concrete was cooled with pipes of cold water. Without them, roughly how long would it have taken to cool? Answer: About 125 years. The Bureau of Reclamation estimated about 125 years; embedded cooling pipes did it in under two. (Hoover Dam, Construction trivia)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=308"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Roughly how long did it take to build the Empire State Building?","x":"Roughly how long did it take to build the Empire State Building? Answer: About 13 months. Built 1930 to 1931 in about 410 days, with steel going up several storeys a week. (Empire State Building, Construction trivia)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=309"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"How tall is the Burj Khalifa?","x":"How tall is the Burj Khalifa? Answer: 828 metres. At 828 m it's the world's tallest building. (Burj Khalifa, Construction trivia)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=310"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"What gave Roman concrete its long life?","x":"What gave Roman concrete its long life? Answer: Volcanic ash (pozzolana) in the mix. Pozzolanic ash reacted with lime to make durable binders; some Roman structures still stand. (Roman concrete, Construction trivia)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=311"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"What's remarkable about the Pantheon's dome in Rome?","x":"What's remarkable about the Pantheon's dome in Rome? Answer: It's the world's largest unreinforced concrete dome. Nearly 1,900 years old and 43 m across, it's still the largest unreinforced concrete dome. (Pantheon, Rome, Construction trivia)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=312"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Who popularized the bar chart for scheduling in the 1910s?","x":"Who popularized the bar chart for scheduling in the 1910s? Answer: Henry Gantt. Henry Gantt's charts showed work against time; they're still everywhere. (Henry Gantt, Project management history)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=313"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Who developed the critical path method in the late 1950s?","x":"Who developed the critical path method in the late 1950s? Answer: DuPont, with Remington Rand. Morgan Walker of DuPont and James Kelley of Remington Rand developed CPM around 1957. (Critical path method, Project management history)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=314"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"PERT was developed for which program?","x":"PERT was developed for which program? Answer: The US Navy's Polaris missile program. PERT came out of the Polaris program in 1958 to handle uncertain durations. (Program evaluation and review technique, Project management history)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=315"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"When was PMI founded?","x":"When was PMI founded? Answer: 1969. PMI was founded in 1969; the first PMBOK Guide followed in 1996. (Project Management Institute, Project management history)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=316"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Where was the Agile Manifesto written in 2001?","x":"Where was the Agile Manifesto written in 2001? Answer: At a ski resort in Snowbird, Utah. Seventeen developers met at Snowbird, Utah, in February 2001. (Agile software development, Project management history)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=317"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"What did Flyvbjerg call the pattern of big projects coming in over budget, late and under benefits, again…","x":"What did Flyvbjerg call the pattern of big projects coming in over budget, late and under benefits, again and again? Answer: The iron law of megaprojects. His 'iron law': over budget, over time, under benefits, over and over again. (Bent Flyvbjerg, Giga projects)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=318"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"In Flyvbjerg's data on thousands of projects, roughly what share delivered on budget, on time and with the…","x":"In Flyvbjerg's data on thousands of projects, roughly what share delivered on budget, on time and with the promised benefits? Answer: About 0.5%. About 8.5% hit cost and schedule; only about 0.5% hit cost, schedule and benefits. (Bent Flyvbjerg, Giga projects)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=319"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"What's reference class forecasting?","x":"What's reference class forecasting? Answer: Forecasting from how similar past projects actually turned out. Looking at a reference class of real outcomes corrects the optimism of inside-view estimates. (Reference class forecasting, Giga projects)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=320"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"The Channel Tunnel opened in 1994. Roughly how far over its budget did it run?","x":"The Channel Tunnel opened in 1994. Roughly how far over its budget did it run? Answer: About 80%. Its construction costs ran about 80% over the estimate, a classic megaproject overrun. (Channel Tunnel, Giga projects)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=321"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Flyvbjerg's advice for big projects: 'Think slow, act ___'.","x":"Flyvbjerg's advice for big projects: 'Think slow, act ___'. Answer: Fast. Plan slowly and thoroughly while change is cheap, then deliver fast, because delivery is when things go wrong. (Bent Flyvbjerg, Giga projects)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=322"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"A surveyor needs centimetre accuracy for setting out anchor bolts. Which GPS method fits?","x":"A surveyor needs centimetre accuracy for setting out anchor bolts. Which GPS method fits? Answer: RTK, with a base station correcting in real time. Real-time kinematic GPS uses a base station's corrections to reach centimetre accuracy. (Global Positioning System, GIS on the job)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=323"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"What's the difference between vector and raster data?","x":"What's the difference between vector and raster data? Answer: Vector is points, lines and shapes; raster is a grid of cells. Vector stores features as geometry; raster stores a value in each grid cell, like elevation or imagery. (Geographic information system, GIS on the job)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=324"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"What's a digital twin of a finished plant?","x":"What's a digital twin of a finished plant? Answer: A live model linked to the real plant's data. A twin connects the model to operating data so the owner can monitor and plan maintenance. (Digital twin, BIM: the model is the job)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=325"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Why does a federated model matter on a big job?","x":"Why does a federated model matter on a big job? Answer: Each discipline keeps its own model, combined for coordination. Federating keeps ownership clear while letting teams see the combined picture. (Building information modeling, BIM: the model is the job)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=326"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"What does 5D BIM add?","x":"What does 5D BIM add? Answer: Cost, from quantities in the model. 5D ties quantities in the model to costs, so changes show their price as they're made. (5D BIM, BIM: the model is the job)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=327"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"What's a 'hold point' in a nuclear inspection plan?","x":"What's a 'hold point' in a nuclear inspection plan? Answer: A step past which work can't go until it's inspected and released. Hold points are mandatory stops; witness points allow work to proceed if the inspector doesn't attend. (Quality assurance, Nuclear QA)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=328"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"A weld defect keeps coming back. What does a good corrective action do?","x":"A weld defect keeps coming back. What does a good corrective action do? Answer: Finds and fixes the root cause so it stops recurring. Corrective action targets the cause; repairs alone treat symptoms. (Corrective and preventive action, Nuclear QA)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=329"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"An inspector finds the torque wrench used all week was out of calibration. What now?","x":"An inspector finds the torque wrench used all week was out of calibration. What now? Answer: Assess and recheck what it was used on since its last good calibration. Everything measured with an out-of-calibration tool since its last good check is suspect until evaluated. (Calibration, Nuclear QA)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=330"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"In Ontario, what training must workers have before using fall protection on construction projects?","x":"In Ontario, what training must workers have before using fall protection on construction projects? Answer: Approved working at heights training. Ontario requires Ministry-approved working at heights training (O. Reg. 297/13) for workers using fall protection on construction projects. (Working at heights, The Green Book (Ontario OHSA))","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=331"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Under Ontario's OHSA, who has the overall duty to ensure a construction project complies?","x":"Under Ontario's OHSA, who has the overall duty to ensure a construction project complies? Answer: The constructor. The constructor is responsible for ensuring the Act and regulations are complied with on the project. (Occupational Health and Safety Act (Ontario), The Green Book (Ontario OHSA))","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=332"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"What makes a supervisor 'competent' under Ontario's OHSA?","x":"What makes a supervisor 'competent' under Ontario's OHSA? Answer: Knowledge, training and experience for the work, knowing the Act and the hazards. The Act defines competent by qualification, training and experience, familiarity with the Act and regulations, and knowledge of the hazards. (Competent person, The Green Book (Ontario OHSA))","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=333"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"What makes a requirement good?","x":"What makes a requirement good? Answer: Clear, testable, needed and traceable. A good requirement says what's needed, can be verified, and links to its source. (Requirements engineering, Systems engineering)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=334"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"What's a single point of failure?","x":"What's a single point of failure? Answer: One part whose failure stops the whole system. Systems engineers hunt single points of failure and add redundancy where the stakes demand it. (Functional safety, Systems engineering)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=335"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Engineers must choose between three pump designs. What tool compares them fairly?","x":"Engineers must choose between three pump designs. What tool compares them fairly? Answer: A trade study with weighted criteria. A trade study scores options against weighted criteria (cost, reliability, maintainability), making the choice transparent. (Trade study, Systems engineering)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=336"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"CANDU reactors produce tritium as a by-product. Where does it end up being useful?","x":"CANDU reactors produce tritium as a by-product. Where does it end up being useful? Answer: In fusion research and glowing exit signs. Tritium from CANDU heavy water is sold for self-luminous signs and fusion research. (Tritium, Nuclear trivia)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=337"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Ontario gets roughly what share of its electricity from nuclear power, in a typical year?","x":"Ontario gets roughly what share of its electricity from nuclear power, in a typical year? Answer: About half or more. Nuclear has supplied around half to 60% of Ontario's electricity in recent years. (Pickering Nuclear Generating Station, Nuclear trivia)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=338"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Besides power, what does Bruce Power's CANDU fleet supply to hospitals worldwide?","x":"Besides power, what does Bruce Power's CANDU fleet supply to hospitals worldwide? Answer: Cobalt-60, for sterilizing equipment and cancer treatment. CANDU reactors produce cobalt-60, used to sterilize medical supplies and in cancer therapy. (Cobalt-60, Nuclear trivia)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=339"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"What made the first attempt to build the Panama Canal fail in the 1880s?","x":"What made the first attempt to build the Panama Canal fail in the 1880s? Answer: Disease and a sea-level design that couldn't work. Yellow fever and malaria killed thousands, and France's sea-level plan proved impractical; the US built a lock canal. (Panama Canal, Construction trivia)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=340"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Why is the Golden Gate Bridge painted 'International Orange'?","x":"Why is the Golden Gate Bridge painted 'International Orange'? Answer: It's visible in fog and fits the setting. The architect chose it for visibility in fog and how it suited the landscape; it was first just the primer. (Golden Gate Bridge, Construction trivia)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=341"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"How was the CN Tower's concrete shaft built so fast?","x":"How was the CN Tower's concrete shaft built so fast? Answer: Slipforming: forms that rise continuously as concrete is poured. Slipform construction raised the shaft continuously, several metres a day. (Slipforming, Construction trivia)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=342"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Which project method came out of UK government use in 1996?","x":"Which project method came out of UK government use in 1996? Answer: PRINCE2. PRINCE2 (Projects in Controlled Environments) was launched by the UK government in 1996. (PRINCE2, Project management history)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=343"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Kanban and lean thinking came from which company?","x":"Kanban and lean thinking came from which company? Answer: Toyota. Kanban grew out of the Toyota Production System in Japan. (Toyota Production System, Project management history)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=344"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Who introduced critical chain project management in 1997?","x":"Who introduced critical chain project management in 1997? Answer: Eliyahu Goldratt. Goldratt's book Critical Chain (1997) applied the theory of constraints to projects, with shared buffers. (Critical chain project management, Project management history)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=345"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Why do big projects so often underestimate cost?","x":"Why do big projects so often underestimate cost? Answer: Optimism and strategic misrepresentation in early estimates. Flyvbjerg's research points to optimism bias and, sometimes, deliberate underestimates to get projects approved. (Optimism bias, Giga projects)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=346"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"What do the best-performing big projects tend to have in common?","x":"What do the best-performing big projects tend to have in common? Answer: Modular, repeatable parts built many times. Repeated modules (like solar and wind farms) let teams learn and scale; bespoke designs carry the most risk. (Modular construction, Giga projects)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=347"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Why did the US nuclear industry set up INPO in 1979?","x":"Why did the US nuclear industry set up INPO in 1979? Answer: After Three Mile Island, so the industry could set and enforce its own standards. Three Mile Island showed the industry it needed to police itself well above the regulatory minimum. INPO was formed by the utilities months later. (Institute of Nuclear Power Operations, INPO and WANO)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=348"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"WANO was founded in 1989. Which accident led to it?","x":"WANO was founded in 1989. Which accident led to it? Answer: Chernobyl. After Chernobyl in 1986, operators worldwide agreed that an accident anywhere is an accident everywhere, and formed WANO to share experience and review each other. (World Association of Nuclear Operators, INPO and WANO)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=349"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Is INPO a regulator?","x":"Is INPO a regulator? Answer: No. It's an industry body; the regulator is the NRC. INPO has no legal power. Its influence comes from peer pressure, confidential ratings, and the fact that every US operator belongs to it. (Institute of Nuclear Power Operations, INPO and WANO)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=350"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"What is a WANO peer review?","x":"What is a WANO peer review? Answer: Experienced people from other plants assess yours against WANO's standards. Peers from other operators spend about two weeks on site, compare the plant with WANO's performance objectives and criteria, and leave areas for improvement. (World Association of Nuclear Operators, INPO and WANO)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=351"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"When does a new unit get its WANO pre-startup peer review?","x":"When does a new unit get its WANO pre-startup peer review? Answer: Before first fuel load. New units are reviewed before they start up, which puts construction, commissioning and the operating organization's readiness under peer scrutiny. (World Association of Nuclear Operators, INPO and WANO)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=352"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"What happens to a plant's INPO evaluation rating?","x":"What happens to a plant's INPO evaluation rating? Answer: It stays confidential, though it's widely reported to affect insurance premiums. Ratings (1 is best) are shared with the utility's leadership, not the public. A poor rating still hurts: peers know, and insurers are reported to factor it in. (Institute of Nuclear Power Operations, INPO and WANO)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=353"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Which is one of INPO's traits of a healthy nuclear safety culture?","x":"Which is one of INPO's traits of a healthy nuclear safety culture? Answer: A questioning attitude. The traits include personal accountability, a questioning attitude, safety communication, leadership safety values, decision-making, a respectful work environment, continuous learning, problem identification and resolution, an environment for raising concerns, and work processes. (Safety culture, INPO and WANO)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=354"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"In human performance, what does the STAR self-check stand for?","x":"In human performance, what does the STAR self-check stand for? Answer: Stop, think, act, review. Before a critical action you stop, think about what should happen, act, then review that it did. It takes seconds and catches the wrong-switch, wrong-valve slip. (Human reliability, INPO and WANO)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=355"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"What does three-way communication mean?","x":"What does three-way communication mean? Answer: The sender says it, the receiver repeats it back, and the sender confirms. Repeat-back plus confirmation catches mishearing before it becomes action, especially on radios and in noisy areas. (Human reliability, INPO and WANO)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=356"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"What should a good pre-job brief cover?","x":"What should a good pre-job brief cover? Answer: The critical steps, what could go wrong, and what to do if it does. A brief focuses on this job today: critical steps, error traps, the stop conditions, and the plan if something goes wrong. (Human reliability, INPO and WANO)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=357"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"What is a significant operating experience report (SOER)?","x":"What is a significant operating experience report (SOER)? Answer: An industry report on serious events, with recommendations members must act on. SOERs turn one plant's bad day into lessons every plant applies, and peer reviews check that members did. (Operating experience, INPO and WANO)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=358"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"What did the Davis-Besse reactor head discovery in 2002 teach the industry?","x":"What did the Davis-Besse reactor head discovery in 2002 teach the industry? Answer: Production pressure can quietly beat safety: boric acid had eaten a cavity in the vessel head. Boric acid leaking for years ate a football-sized cavity through the carbon steel head, leaving only thin stainless cladding. Warning signs had been explained away to keep the plant running. (Davis-Besse Nuclear Power Station, INPO and WANO)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=359"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"INPO's human performance model groups error precursors into four kinds. Which list is right?","x":"INPO's human performance model groups error precursors into four kinds. Which list is right? Answer: Task demands, individual capabilities, work environment, human nature. Spotting precursors (a rushed task, an unfamiliar crew member, a noisy area, overconfidence) before the job is what lets you set defences against error. (Human reliability, INPO and WANO)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=360"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Why should a construction manager on a new nuclear unit care about WANO and INPO operating experience?","x":"Why should a construction manager on a new nuclear unit care about WANO and INPO operating experience? Answer: Other new units' lessons, like first-of-a-kind problems, let you avoid repeating them. Operating experience includes construction and commissioning lessons. Reading it before you start is the cheapest risk response there is. (World Association of Nuclear Operators, INPO and WANO)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=361"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"Where is WANO's coordinating centre?","x":"Where is WANO's coordinating centre? Answer: London. WANO is coordinated from London, with regional centres including Atlanta, Moscow, Paris and Tokyo. (World Association of Nuclear Operators, INPO and WANO)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=362"},{"k":"Bootcamp question","t":"What does the National Academy for Nuclear Training do?","x":"What does the National Academy for Nuclear Training do? Answer: Accredits US plants' training programs through an independent board. Part of INPO, it sets the standard for training. An independent board accredits each plant's programs, and the NRC recognizes accreditation. (National Academy for Nuclear Training, INPO and WANO)","u":"/pmp-field-drill/#q=363"},{"k":"Scope Creep lesson","t":"To-do list","x":"Writing tasks down frees your head for doing them. Every plan starts as a list. Quiz: What is the first thing a to-do list gives you? A record you can check instead of remembering","u":"/scope-creep/"},{"k":"Scope Creep lesson","t":"Shopping list","x":"Procurement is getting what you need, when you need it, at a fair price. Buy before you run out. Quiz: Lemons run out every Friday afternoon. What fixes it? Order based on how fast you use them","u":"/scope-creep/"},{"k":"Scope Creep lesson","t":"Check the weather","x":"A risk is something that might happen and would affect the project. Plan for it before it happens. Quiz: Which of these is a risk, not an issue? Rain is forecast for tomorrow","u":"/scope-creep/"},{"k":"Scope Creep lesson","t":"Gantt chart","x":"A Gantt chart shows tasks as bars on a calendar, so everyone can see what happens when. Quiz: What does the length of a bar on a Gantt chart show? How long the task takes","u":"/scope-creep/"},{"k":"Scope Creep lesson","t":"Kanban board","x":"Kanban makes work visible in columns (to do, doing, done) and limits how much is in progress at once. Quiz: Your \"doing\" column is overflowing. What does Kanban say? Finish work in progress before starting new work","u":"/scope-creep/"},{"k":"Scope Creep lesson","t":"Food safety plan","x":"Quality is planned in, not inspected in. A plan prevents problems that checks only find. Quiz: Which is cheaper, usually? Preventing a defect","u":"/scope-creep/"},{"k":"Scope Creep lesson","t":"Standard work","x":"Standard work is the current best way to do a task, written down, so everyone does it the same way and improves it together. Quiz: Why write down the best way to make a latte? So every store gets the same result and can improve it","u":"/scope-creep/"},{"k":"Scope Creep lesson","t":"Customer survey","x":"Stakeholders are anyone affected by your project. Find out what they need early. Quiz: When is the best time to engage stakeholders? Early, and all the way through","u":"/scope-creep/"},{"k":"Scope Creep lesson","t":"Loyalty program","x":"Benefits are the reason for a project: what improves for the business after delivery. Quiz: A loyalty program launches on time. Is the project a success yet? Not until the benefits, like repeat customers, show up","u":"/scope-creep/"},{"k":"Scope Creep lesson","t":"Critical path method","x":"The critical path is the longest chain of dependent tasks. It sets the earliest finish date. Delay it and the project is late. Quiz: Which task should get the scarce crane first? The one on the critical path","u":"/scope-creep/"},{"k":"Scope Creep lesson","t":"Safety program","x":"STKY: the stuff that kills you. Put the most attention on hazards that can cause serious injury or death. Quiz: Which hazard deserves the most attention? A suspended load over a walkway","u":"/scope-creep/"},{"k":"Scope Creep lesson","t":"Change control","x":"Every change to scope, cost or schedule is logged, its impact assessed, and approved before anyone does it. Quiz: The owner asks for an extra floor. What happens first? Assess the impact on cost, schedule and risk","u":"/scope-creep/"},{"k":"Scope Creep lesson","t":"Earned value","x":"Earned value compares the value of work done (EV) with what it cost (AC) and what was planned (PV). CPI = EV ÷ AC. Quiz: EV is $80k and AC is $100k. What is CPI? 0.8, over budget","u":"/scope-creep/"},{"k":"Scope Creep lesson","t":"Advanced work packaging","x":"AWP plans backwards from the crews: a crew only gets a work package once every constraint (drawings, materials, scaffold, permits) is cleared. Quiz: A work package is missing its scaffold. What do you do? Hold it and release one that is ready","u":"/scope-creep/"},{"k":"Scope Creep lesson","t":"BIM model","x":"Building information modelling is a shared 3D model with data. Clashes are found on screen, not in the field. Quiz: Where is the cheapest place to find a pipe hitting a beam? In the model, before construction","u":"/scope-creep/"},{"k":"Scope Creep lesson","t":"Nuclear QA program","x":"Nuclear QA means everything important to safety is controlled and recorded. Every weld, every test, every signature is traceable. Quiz: A gauge was out of calibration. What about work measured with it? Treat it as suspect and re-check it","u":"/scope-creep/"},{"k":"Scope Creep lesson","t":"Human performance tools","x":"Self-checking (stop, think, act, review), peer checks and three-way communication catch human slips before they matter. Quiz: What is three-way communication? Say it, the listener repeats it, you confirm","u":"/scope-creep/"},{"k":"Scope Creep lesson","t":"Peer review","x":"Peer reviews bring experienced people from other sites to find what you have stopped seeing. In nuclear, WANO and INPO run them. Quiz: What is a peer review for? Finding problems you have stopped seeing","u":"/scope-creep/"},{"k":"Scope Creep lesson","t":"Systems engineering","x":"The V-model: requirements go down the left side to design, and tests come back up the right side to prove each requirement is met. Quiz: Verification asks \"did we build it right?\" What does validation ask? Did we build the right thing?","u":"/scope-creep/"},{"k":"Scope Creep lesson","t":"Launch readiness review","x":"A readiness review is a go or no-go decision based on data, made before the point of no return. Quiz: When should a readiness review happen? Before the point of no return","u":"/scope-creep/"},{"k":"Scope Creep lesson","t":"Digital twin","x":"A digital twin is a live model linked to real data from the asset, so you can test changes safely. Quiz: What makes a model a digital twin? It is linked to live data from the real thing","u":"/scope-creep/"},{"k":"Scope Creep lesson","t":"Last Planner system","x":"The people doing the work plan the next week and promise only what they can deliver. Track how many promises were kept. Quiz: Who makes the weekly work plan in Last Planner? The people who will do the work","u":"/scope-creep/"},{"k":"Scope Creep lesson","t":"Modular construction","x":"Build modules in a factory where conditions are controlled, then assemble on site. Less weather, better quality. Quiz: What is the main advantage of building modules off site? Controlled conditions and less site work","u":"/scope-creep/"},{"k":"Scope Creep lesson","t":"Reference class forecasting","x":"Estimate by looking at how similar past projects actually turned out, not just at your own plan. Quiz: How do you use reference class forecasting? Look at how similar past projects turned out","u":"/scope-creep/"},{"k":"Scope Creep lesson","t":"Portfolio management","x":"A portfolio is all the projects an organization chooses to do. Pick the ones that best fit the strategy, and stop the rest. Quiz: Twelve projects, money for six. What does portfolio management do? Rank them by strategic value and fund the best","u":"/scope-creep/"},{"k":"Scope Creep lesson","t":"Program governance","x":"Governance is how decisions get made: who decides, on what, and when. Good governance decides fast and writes it down. Quiz: What is the main job of a program board? Make the big decisions and own them","u":"/scope-creep/"},{"k":"Scope Creep lesson","t":"Morale officer","x":"Teams go through forming, storming, norming and performing. Leaders adjust their style to the stage. Quiz: A new team argues about how to work. Which stage is it? Storming","u":"/scope-creep/"},{"k":"Scope Creep lesson","t":"AI scheduler","x":"Tools can re-plan fast, but a schedule is only as good as its logic and its data. Garbage in, garbage out, at the speed of light. Quiz: An AI schedule says a task takes zero days. What do you do? Check the data and the logic","u":"/scope-creep/"},{"k":"Scope Creep lesson","t":"Self-replicating crews","x":"Developing people is part of the job. Coaching and cross-training make the team stronger than any one person. Quiz: Your only welder for an alloy is going on leave. What should have happened earlier? Cross-train a second welder","u":"/scope-creep/"},{"k":"Scope Creep lesson","t":"Monte Carlo everything","x":"Monte Carlo simulation runs the schedule thousands of times with random variation, giving a range of finish dates and their odds. Quiz: What does a Monte Carlo schedule give you? A range of finish dates with probabilities","u":"/scope-creep/"},{"k":"Scope Creep lesson","t":"Galactic PMO","x":"A project management office sets standards, collects status and helps projects succeed. The good ones make reporting lighter, not heavier. Quiz: What should a good PMO do to reporting? Make it lighter and generated from the plan","u":"/scope-creep/"},{"k":"Scope Creep lesson","t":"Hyperspace supply chain","x":"Track every item at every handoff. If a crew has to ask where material is, the tracking is missing a step. Quiz: Material shows \"shipped\" but never arrives. What is missing? A scan at the receiving handoff","u":"/scope-creep/"},{"k":"Scope Creep lesson","t":"Benefits realization","x":"Benefits are measured after delivery, against the business case. Someone in the business owns each benefit. Quiz: Who usually owns a benefit after the project ends? Someone in the business that uses it","u":"/scope-creep/"},{"k":"Scope Creep lesson","t":"The RFI pile","x":"RFIs are questions that block work. Rank them by what they release, not by when they arrived. Forty requests for information are sitting unanswered. Crews are working around them.","u":"/scope-creep/"},{"k":"Scope Creep lesson","t":"Long-lead equipment","x":"Long-lead items set the schedule. Order them as soon as the design is stable enough, not when it's perfect. The main transformer has a 60-week lead time. Design is 80% done.","u":"/scope-creep/"},{"k":"Scope Creep lesson","t":"Concrete cylinders failed","x":"Early test results are a warning, not a verdict. Hold what depends on it, get the engineer, and wait for the real data. The 7-day tests on Tuesday's pour came back low. Formwork above is due to start.","u":"/scope-creep/"},{"k":"Scope Creep lesson","t":"Toolbox talk or production?","x":"A short talk about today's hazards beats a long one about everything. It's the conversation that matters, not the signature. The super wants to skip Monday's safety talk because the schedule is tight.","u":"/scope-creep/"},{"k":"Scope Creep lesson","t":"Liquidated damages","x":"Liquidated damages are a pre-agreed price for lateness. Your records decide who really caused each day. You're three weeks behind. The contract charges $50k a day for late finish.","u":"/scope-creep/"},{"k":"Scope Creep lesson","t":"Neighbours are angry","x":"Stakeholders you ignore become constraints. Engage them early and they often bring the fix. Night pours are keeping the neighbourhood awake. A petition is circulating.","u":"/scope-creep/"},{"k":"Scope Creep lesson","t":"Three-week lookahead","x":"The lookahead is where the plan meets the work. Constraints found there should flow back into the master schedule. The master schedule says one thing, the whiteboard says another.","u":"/scope-creep/"},{"k":"Scope Creep lesson","t":"The apprentice has an idea","x":"Good ideas come from anywhere. Test small, measure, and give credit where it's earned. An apprentice suggests a jig that would halve framing time.","u":"/scope-creep/"},{"k":"Scope Creep lesson","t":"Winter concreting","x":"Cold weather concreting is about protecting the concrete while it cures: heat, insulation and monitoring. Tonight drops to −15 °C. Tomorrow's pour is a big slab.","u":"/scope-creep/"},{"k":"Scope Creep lesson","t":"Cash flow crunch","x":"Cash is the blood of a contractor. Clean, on-time progress billing gets paid; sloppy billing waits. The owner is paying 60 days late. Payroll is Friday.","u":"/scope-creep/"},{"k":"Scope Creep lesson","t":"Punch list explosion","x":"A punch list is easiest to beat by owner, area and photo proof. Better yet, build it right the first time. At substantial completion, the owner's walkthrough found 1,400 items.","u":"/scope-creep/"},{"k":"Scope Creep lesson","t":"Counterfeit valve","x":"Suspect items are quarantined and reported, not guessed at. Verify with the original manufacturer. A safety-related valve arrived with a certificate that looks photocopied.","u":"/scope-creep/"},{"k":"Scope Creep lesson","t":"Fast-track or crash?","x":"Crashing adds people (costs money). Fast-tracking overlaps work (adds risk). Only critical work makes the job shorter. The owner wants the job two weeks sooner.","u":"/scope-creep/"},{"k":"Scope Creep lesson","t":"Union steward is unhappy","x":"Jurisdictional disputes have a process. Follow it before anyone walks. Two trades both claim the cable tray work.","u":"/scope-creep/"},{"k":"Scope Creep lesson","t":"Hold point with no inspector","x":"A hold point stops work until it's released by the right person. Plan for the inspector being away. The inspector is sick. The hold point blocks two crews.","u":"/scope-creep/"},{"k":"Scope Creep lesson","t":"Lightning in the area","x":"When thunder roars, go indoors. Stop lifts and high work until 30 minutes after the last thunder. Thunder is ten seconds after the flash. The crane is up.","u":"/scope-creep/"},{"k":"Scope Creep lesson","t":"Value engineering","x":"Value engineering keeps the function and lowers the cost. Cutting scope or safety is not value engineering. The budget is 8% over. The owner wants savings.","u":"/scope-creep/"},{"k":"Scope Creep lesson","t":"Superintendent burned out","x":"People are the project. Protect your key people, and build backup for every critical role. Your best super has worked 60-hour weeks for four months.","u":"/scope-creep/"},{"k":"Scope Creep lesson","t":"The regulator wants a meeting","x":"With a regulator, openness builds trust. They already know more than you think. The nuclear regulator wants to discuss your quality program.","u":"/scope-creep/"},{"k":"Scope Creep lesson","t":"Material escalation","x":"Read the contract before panicking. Escalation clauses and early buying are the usual tools. Steel prices jumped 30% since you bid.","u":"/scope-creep/"},{"k":"Scope Creep lesson","t":"Supply ship missed the window","x":"When a delivery slips, re-sequence around what you have. Never install untested substitutes. The cargo ship to the base missed its launch window. Next one is in 26 days.","u":"/scope-creep/"},{"k":"Scope Creep lesson","t":"Pressure test on the habitat","x":"A failed test means find the cause, not repeat the test. Nobody moves in until it passes. The new dome lost 2% pressure overnight.","u":"/scope-creep/"},{"k":"Scope Creep lesson","t":"Crew wants to go home","x":"Fatigue is a hazard. Rotations and rest are part of the plan, especially far from home. The crew has been in orbit for nine months. Rotation is overdue.","u":"/scope-creep/"},{"k":"Scope Creep lesson","t":"Colonists want a say","x":"The people who live with the result often know the site best. Ask them early. The colony council wants input on where the new mine goes.","u":"/scope-creep/"},{"k":"Scope Creep lesson","t":"Change order pricing","x":"Price changes transparently, from real quantities and real risks. Never start work on an unpriced change. The owner wants a new launch bay. You need to price it.","u":"/scope-creep/"},{"k":"Scope Creep lesson","t":"Near miss reported","x":"A near miss is a free lesson. Thank the reporter and fix the system, or people stop reporting. A worker reports that a hammer fell three floors and missed someone.","u":"/scope-creep/"},{"k":"Scope Creep lesson","t":"Built to the wrong drawing","x":"Fix the problem, then fix the process that let the wrong drawing reach the crew. A crew installed pipe to Rev B. Rev D moved it 600 mm.","u":"/scope-creep/"},{"k":"Scope Creep lesson","t":"Permit delay","x":"Ask regulators what they need, then give it to them fast. Work around the constraint, never through it. The city says the building permit needs another month.","u":"/scope-creep/"},{"k":"Scope Creep lesson","t":"Two foremen are feuding","x":"Conflict is usually about the work, not the people. Collaborate on the real issue. They won't talk to each other, and their crews share a floor.","u":"/scope-creep/"},{"k":"Scope Creep lesson","t":"Heat wave","x":"Heat stress is preventable: earlier hours, shade, water, rest and buddies watching for the signs. The humidex is 42. Crews are on the roof.","u":"/scope-creep/"},{"k":"Scope Creep lesson","t":"Retention money","x":"Retention is often a contractor's whole margin. Closing out properly is how you get paid. The owner is holding 10% until a list of small items is closed.","u":"/scope-creep/"},{"k":"Scope Creep lesson","t":"Journalist at the gate","x":"With the public, say what you know, what you're doing, and when you'll know more. A reporter asks about a rumour of cracks in the structure.","u":"/scope-creep/"},{"k":"Scope Creep lesson","t":"Safety walk: the frame","x":"The energy wheel: gravity is the top killer on construction sites. Edges first. You walk the third floor. Which one could kill someone?","u":"/scope-creep/"},{"k":"Scope Creep lesson","t":"Safety walk: the lift","x":"Suspended loads are high energy. Nobody under the load, ever. The crane is flying steel. Which one could kill someone?","u":"/scope-creep/"},{"k":"Scope Creep lesson","t":"Safety walk: the trench","x":"Trench walls hold tonnes of soil. Over 1.2 m, they need support. A crew is laying pipe. Which one could kill someone?","u":"/scope-creep/"},{"k":"Scope Creep lesson","t":"Safety walk: the switchgear room","x":"Electrical energy kills without warning. Isolate, lock, tag and verify. Electricians are working. Which one could kill someone?","u":"/scope-creep/"},{"k":"Scope Creep lesson","t":"Safety walk: the tank","x":"Confined spaces kill would-be rescuers too. Always test the air before entry. A crew is about to enter a tank. Which one could kill someone?","u":"/scope-creep/"},{"k":"Scope Creep lesson","t":"Safety walk: the excavator","x":"Mobile equipment kills by contact. Keep people out of the swing and travel paths. A machine is digging. Which one could kill someone?","u":"/scope-creep/"},{"k":"Scope Creep lesson","t":"Safety walk: the airlock","x":"Pressure is stored energy. Interlocks exist so one mistake can't kill everyone. You inspect the new habitat. Which one could kill someone?","u":"/scope-creep/"},{"k":"Scope Creep lesson","t":"Safety walk: radiography","x":"Radiation is invisible energy. Distance, time and shielding. A radiography crew is shooting welds. Which one could kill someone?","u":"/scope-creep/"},{"k":"Scope Creep lesson","t":"Make lemonade properly","x":"Every process has a sequence. Getting it right the first time is quality. Tap the steps in the right order.","u":"/scope-creep/"},{"k":"Scope Creep lesson","t":"Open the food truck","x":"Work has logic: some things must finish before others can start. Tap the steps in the right order.","u":"/scope-creep/"},{"k":"Scope Creep lesson","t":"Pour a slab","x":"This is the critical path in miniature: each step depends on the one before. Tap the steps in the right order.","u":"/scope-creep/"},{"k":"Scope Creep lesson","t":"Lock out a machine","x":"Lockout only works in order. Verifying zero energy is the step people skip, and the one that saves them. Tap the steps in the right order.","u":"/scope-creep/"},{"k":"Scope Creep lesson","t":"Make a crane lift","x":"A lift is planned long before the hook moves. Tap the steps in the right order.","u":"/scope-creep/"},{"k":"Scope Creep lesson","t":"Answer an RFI","x":"An RFI isn't closed until the drawings change. Otherwise the next crew asks again. Tap the steps in the right order.","u":"/scope-creep/"},{"k":"Scope Creep lesson","t":"Process a change","x":"Integrated change control: log, assess, decide, update, communicate. Tap the steps in the right order.","u":"/scope-creep/"},{"k":"Scope Creep lesson","t":"Commission a system","x":"Commissioning proves the system works before the owner takes it. Tap the steps in the right order.","u":"/scope-creep/"},{"k":"Scope Creep lesson","t":"Launch a payload","x":"A readiness review comes before the point of no return. Tap the steps in the right order.","u":"/scope-creep/"},{"k":"Scope Creep lesson","t":"Build a dome","x":"Testing comes before occupancy. Always. Tap the steps in the right order.","u":"/scope-creep/"},{"k":"Scope Creep lesson","t":"Dig near a utility","x":"Locate, expose, then dig. Utility strikes happen when the order breaks. Tap the steps in the right order.","u":"/scope-creep/"},{"k":"Scope Creep lesson","t":"Run a good meeting","x":"A meeting without owners and dates is just a conversation. Tap the steps in the right order.","u":"/scope-creep/"},{"k":"Scope Creep lesson","t":"Role: Site cook","x":"Looking after the crew is part of the plan, not a perk. Rested, fed people make fewer mistakes.","u":"/scope-creep/"},{"k":"Scope Creep lesson","t":"Role: Foreman","x":"A foreman turns the plan into the day's work and keeps the crew moving, so the manager can look ahead.","u":"/scope-creep/"},{"k":"Scope Creep lesson","t":"Role: Buyer","x":"Procurement gets what the job needs, when it needs it, at the best value. Not the cheapest: the best value.","u":"/scope-creep/"},{"k":"Scope Creep lesson","t":"Role: Job coordinator","x":"A project coordinator keeps the paperwork, follow-ups and loose ends moving so nothing finished sits unclaimed.","u":"/scope-creep/"},{"k":"Scope Creep lesson","t":"Role: Opportunity scout","x":"Opportunities are risks with an upside. The responses are exploit, enhance, share and accept.","u":"/scope-creep/"},{"k":"Scope Creep lesson","t":"Role: Risk manager","x":"Every risk needs an owner: the person best placed to watch for it and act when it happens.","u":"/scope-creep/"}];
